\id 1SA - Berean Standard Bible
\h 1 Samuel
\toc2 1 Samuel
\toc1 1 Samuel
\mt1 1 Samuel
\c 1
\s1 Elkanah and His Wives
\r (Psalms 113:1–9)
\m
\v 1 Now there was \zwd a man named Elkanah|Q2424977\zwd* who was from Ramathaim-zophim \f + \fr 1:1 \ft Or from Ramathaim, a Zuphite; see LXX and 1 Chronicles 6:26 and 35.\f* in the hill country of Ephraim. He was the son of \zwd Jeroham|id="Q6182499" t="2"\zwd*, the son of \zwd Elihu|id="Q115052422" t="2"\zwd*,\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Elihu is also called Eliab and Eliel; see 1 Chronicles 6:27 and 34.\f* the son of \zwd Tohu|id="Q115052531" t="2"\zwd*, the son of \zwd Zuph|id="Q115052712" t="2"\zwd*, an Ephraimite.
\v 2 He had \zwd two wives|Q2346367,Q2068934\zwd*, one named \zwd Hannah|Q2346367\zwd* and the other \zwd Peninnah|Q2068934\zwd*. And \zwd Peninnah|Q2068934\zwd* had children, but \zwd Hannah|Q2346367\zwd* had none.
\b
\m
\v 3 Year after year \zwd Elkanah|Q2424977\zwd* would go up from his city to worship and sacrifice to the LORD of Hosts at Shiloh, where \zwd Eli|id="Q362021" t="2"\zwd*’s two sons, \zwd Hophni|Q1563312\zwd* and \zwd Phinehas|Q20723908\zwd*, were priests to the LORD.
\v 4 And whenever the day came for \zwd Elkanah|Q2424977\zwd* to present his sacrifice, he would give portions to \zwd his wife Peninnah|Q2068934\zwd* and to all her sons and daughters.
\v 5 But to \zwd Hannah|Q2346367\zwd* he would give a double portion,\f + \fr 1:5 \ft Or a choice portion\f* for he loved her even though the LORD had closed her womb.
\b
\m
\v 6 Because the LORD had closed \zwd Hannah|Q2346367\zwd*’s womb, her rival would provoke her viciously to taunt her.
\v 7 And this went on year after year. Whenever \zwd Hannah|Q2346367\zwd* went up to the house of the LORD, her rival taunted her until she wept and would not eat.
\b
\m
\v 8 “\zwd Hannah|Q2346367\zwd*, why are you crying?” \zwd her husband Elkanah|Q2424977\zwd* asked. “Why won’t you eat? Why is your heart so grieved? Am I not better to you than ten sons?”
\s1 Hannah Prays for a Son
\b
\m
\v 9 So after they had finished eating and drinking in Shiloh, \zwd Hannah|Q2346367\zwd* stood up. Now \zwd Eli the priest|Q362021\zwd* was sitting on a chair by the doorpost of the temple of the LORD.
\b
\m
\v 10 In her bitter distress, \zwd Hannah|Q2346367\zwd* prayed to the LORD and wept with many tears.
\v 11 And she made a vow, saying, “O LORD of Hosts, if only You will look upon the affliction of \zwd Your maidservant|Q2346367\zwd* and remember me, not forgetting \zwd Your maidservant|Q2346367\zwd* but giving her a son, then I will dedicate him to the LORD all the days of his life, and no razor shall ever touch his head.”
\b
\m
\v 12 As \zwd Hannah|Q2346367\zwd* kept on praying before the LORD, \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd* watched her mouth.
\v 13 \zwd Hannah|Q2346367\zwd* was praying in her heart, and though her lips were moving, her voice could not be heard.
\b
\m So \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd* thought she was drunk
\v 14 and said to her, “How long will you be drunk? Put away your wine!”
\b
\m
\v 15 “No, \zwd my lord|Q362021\zwd*,” \zwd Hannah|Q2346367\zwd* replied. “I am a woman troubled in spirit. I have not had any wine or strong drink, but I have poured out my soul before the LORD.
\v 16 Do not take \zwd your servant|Q2346367\zwd* for a wicked woman, for all this time I have been praying out of the depth of my anguish and grief.”
\b
\m
\v 17 “Go in peace,” \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd* replied, “and may the God of Israel grant the petition you have asked of Him.”
\b
\m
\v 18 “May \zwd your maidservant|Q2346367\zwd* find favor with you,” said \zwd Hannah|Q2346367\zwd*. Then she went on her way, and she began to eat, and her face was no longer downcast.
\s1 The Birth of Samuel
\b
\m
\v 19 The next morning they got up early to bow in worship before the LORD, and then they returned home to Ramah.
\b
\m \zdate|Q6577515:P569+P-9M\*And \zwd Elkanah|Q2424977\zwd* had relations with \zwd his wife Hannah|Q2346367\zwd*, and the LORD remembered her.
\v 20 \zdate|Q6577515:P569\*So in the course of time, \zwd Hannah|Q2346367\zwd* conceived and gave birth to \zwd a son|Q6577515\zwd*. She named him \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*,\f + \fr 1:20 \ft Samuel sounds like the Hebrew for heard of God.\f* saying, “Because I have asked for him from the LORD.”
\b
\m
\v 21 Then \zwd Elkanah|Q2424977\zwd* and all his house went up to make the annual sacrifice to the LORD and to fulfill his vow,
\v 22 but \zwd Hannah|Q2346367\zwd* did not go. “After \zwd the boy|Q6577515\zwd* is weaned,” she said to \zwd her husband|Q2424977\zwd*, “I will take him to appear before the LORD and to stay there permanently.”\f + \fr 1:22 \ft MT; DSS include I will offer him as a Nazirite for all time.\f*
\b
\m
\v 23 “Do what you think is best,” \zwd her husband Elkanah|Q2424977\zwd* replied, “and stay here until you have weaned him. Only may the LORD confirm His word.”\f + \fr 1:23 \ft MT; DSS, LXX, and Syriac your word\f*
\b
\m So \zwd Hannah|Q2346367\zwd* stayed and nursed \zwd her son|Q6577515\zwd* until she had weaned him.
\b
\m
\v 24 Once she had weaned him, \zwd Hannah|Q2346367\zwd* took \zwd the boy|Q6577515\zwd* with her, along with a three-year-old bull,\f + \fr 1:24 \ft DSS, LXX, and Syriac; MT three bulls\f* an ephah of flour,\f + \fr 1:24 \ft An ephah is approximately 20 dry quarts or 22 liters (probably about 25.5 pounds or 11.6 kilograms of flour).\f* and a skin of wine. Though \zwd the boy|Q6577515\zwd* was still young, she brought him to the house of the LORD at Shiloh.
\v 25 And when they had slaughtered the bull, they brought \zwd the boy|Q6577515\zwd* to \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd*.
\b
\m
\v 26 “Please, \zwd my lord|Q362021\zwd*,” said \zwd Hannah|Q2346367\zwd*, “as surely as you live, \zwd my lord|\zwd*, I am \zwd the woman|Q2346367\zwd* who stood here beside you praying to the LORD.
\v 27 I prayed for \zwd this boy|Q6577515\zwd*, and since the LORD has granted me what I asked of Him,
\v 28 I now dedicate \zwd the boy|Q6577515\zwd* to the LORD. For as long as he lives, he is dedicated to the LORD.”
\b
\m So they worshiped \f + \fr 1:28 \ft One DSS manuscript; MT he worshiped\f* the LORD there.
\c 2
\s1 Hannah’s Prayer of Thanksgiving
\r (Luke 1:46–56)
\m 
\v 1 At that time \zwd Hannah|Q2346367\zwd* prayed: 
\b
\q1 “My heart rejoices in the LORD; 
\q2 my horn \f + \fr 2:1 \ft Or strength; also in verse 10\f* is exalted in the LORD. 
\q1 My mouth speaks boldly against my enemies, 
\q2 for I rejoice in Your salvation. 
\q1 
\v 2 There is no one holy like the LORD. 
\q2 Indeed, there is no one besides You! 
\q2 And there is no Rock like our God. 
\b
\q1 
\v 3 Do not boast so proudly, 
\q2 or let arrogance come from your mouth, 
\q1 for the LORD is a God who knows, 
\q2 and by Him actions are weighed. 
\b
\q1 
\v 4 The bows of the mighty are broken, 
\q2 but the feeble are equipped with strength. 
\q1 
\v 5 The well-fed hire themselves out for food, 
\q2 but the starving hunger no more. 
\q1 The barren woman gives birth to seven, 
\q2 but she who has many sons pines away. 
\b
\q1 
\v 6 The LORD brings death and gives life; 
\q2 He brings down to Sheol and raises up. 
\q1 
\v 7 The LORD sends poverty and wealth; 
\q2 He humbles and He exalts. 
\q1 
\v 8 He raises the poor from the dust 
\q2 and lifts the needy from the ash heap. 
\q1 He seats them among princes 
\q2 and bestows on them a throne of honor. 
\b
\q1 For the foundations of the earth are the LORD’s, 
\q2 and upon them He has set the world. 
\q1 
\v 9 He guards the steps of His faithful ones, 
\q2 but the wicked perish in darkness; 
\q2 for by his own strength shall no man prevail. 
\b
\q1 
\v 10 Those who oppose the LORD will be shattered. 
\q2 He will thunder from heaven against them. 
\q1 The LORD will judge the ends of the earth 
\q2 and will give power to His king. 
\q2 He will exalt the horn of His anointed.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 Then \zwd Elkanah|Q2424977\zwd* went home to Ramah, but \zwd the boy|Q6577515\zwd* began ministering to the LORD before \zwd Eli the priest|Q362021\zwd*. 
\s1 Eli’s Wicked Sons
\b
\m 
\v 12 Now \zwd the sons of \zwd Eli|id="Q362021" t="2"\zwd*|Q1563312,Q20723908\zwd* were wicked men; they had no regard for the LORD 
\v 13 or for the custom of the priests with the people. 
\b
\m When any man offered a sacrifice, the servant of the priest would come with a three-pronged meat fork while the meat was boiling 
\v 14 and plunge it into the pan or kettle or cauldron or cooking pot. And the priest would claim for himself whatever the meat fork brought up. This is how they treated all the Israelites who came to Shiloh. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 Even before the fat was burned, the servant of the priest would come and say to the man who was sacrificing, “Give the priest some meat to roast, because he will not accept boiled meat from you, but only raw.” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 And if any man said to him, “The fat must be burned first; then you may take whatever you want,” the servant would reply, “No, you must give it to me right now. If you refuse, I will take it by force!” 
\b
\m 
\v 17 Thus the sin of \zwd these young men|Q1563312,Q20723908\zwd* was severe in the sight of the LORD, for they \f + \fr 2:17 \ft DSS and LXX; MT men\f* were treating the LORD’s offering with contempt. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 Now \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* was ministering before the LORD—\zwd a boy|Q6577515\zwd* wearing a linen ephod. 
\v 19 Each year \zwd his mother|Q2346367\zwd* would make him a little robe and bring it to him when she went with \zwd her husband|Q2424977\zwd* to offer the annual sacrifice. 
\v 20 And \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd* would bless \zwd Elkanah|Q2424977\zwd* and \zwd his wife|Q2346367\zwd*, saying, “May the LORD give you children by \zwd this woman|Q2346367\zwd* in place of the one she dedicated to the LORD.\f + \fr 2:20 \ft DSS; MT in place of the one requested from the LORD\f*” Then they would go home. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 So the LORD attended to \zwd Hannah|Q2346367\zwd*, and she conceived and gave birth to three sons and two daughters. 
\b
\m Meanwhile, \zwd the boy Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* grew up in the presence of the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 Now \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd* was very old, and he heard about everything \zwd his sons|Q1563312,Q20723908\zwd* were doing to all Israel and how they were sleeping with the women who served at the entrance to the Tent of Meeting. 
\b
\m 
\v 23 “Why are you doing these things?” \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd* said to \zwd his sons|Q1563312,Q20723908\zwd*. “I hear about your wicked deeds from all these people. 
\v 24 No, \zwd my sons|Q1563312,Q20723908\zwd*; it is not a good report I hear circulating among the LORD’s people. 
\v 25 If a man sins against another man, God \f + \fr 2:25 \ft Or the judges\f* can intercede for him; but if a man sins against the LORD, who can intercede for him?” 
\b
\m But they would not listen to \zwd their father|Q362021\zwd*, since the LORD intended to put them to death. 
\b
\m 
\v 26 And \zwd the boy Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* continued to grow in stature and in favor with the LORD and with man. 
\s1 A Prophecy against the House of Eli
\b
\m 
\v 27 Then a man of God came to \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd* and told him, “This is what the LORD says: ‘Did I not clearly reveal Myself to \zwd your father|\zwd*’s house when they were in Egypt under Pharaoh’s house? 
\v 28 And out of all the tribes of Israel I selected \zwd your father|\zwd* to be My priest, to offer sacrifices on My altar, to burn incense, and to wear an ephod in My presence. I also gave to the house of \zwd your father|\zwd* all the food offerings of the Israelites. 
\b
\m 
\v 29 Why then do you kick at \f + \fr 2:29 \ft Or scorn\f* My sacrifice and offering that I have prescribed for My dwelling place? You have honored \zwd your sons|Q1563312,Q20723908\zwd* more than Me by fattening yourselves with the best of all the offerings of My people Israel.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 30 Therefore, the LORD, the God of Israel, declares: 
\b
\q1 ‘I did indeed say that your house 
\q2 and the house of \zwd your father|\zwd* 
\q2 would walk before Me forever. 
\b
\q1 But now the LORD declares: 
\q2 Far be it from Me! 
\q1 For I will honor those who honor Me, 
\q2 but those who despise Me will be disdained. 
\b
\m 
\v 31 Behold, the days are coming when I will cut off your strength and the strength of \zwd your father|\zwd*’s house, so that no one in it will reach old age. 
\v 32 You will see distress in My dwelling place. Despite all that is good in Israel, no one in your house will ever again reach old age. 
\v 33 And every one of you that I do not cut off from My altar, I will cause your eyes to fail and your heart to grieve.\f + \fr 2:33 \ft Hebrew; LXX his eyes will fail and his heart will grieve\f* All your descendants \f + \fr 2:33 \ft Or increase \f* will die by the sword of men.\f + \fr 2:33 \ft DSS and LXX; MT will die as mortals or will die in the prime of life\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 34 And this sign shall come to you concerning your two sons \zwd Hophni|Q1563312\zwd* and \zwd Phinehas|Q20723908\zwd*: They will both die on the same day. 
\b
\m 
\v 35 Then I will raise up for Myself \zwd a faithful priest|\zwd*. He will do whatever is in My heart and mind. And I will build for him an enduring house, and he will walk before My anointed one for all time. 
\b
\m 
\v 36 And everyone left in your house will come and bow down to him for a piece of silver or a morsel of bread, pleading, “Please appoint me to some priestly office so that I can eat a piece of bread.”’” 
\c 3
\s1 The LORD Calls Samuel
\m 
\v 1 And \zwd the boy Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* ministered to the LORD before \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd*. 
\b
\m Now in those days the word of the LORD was rare, and visions were scarce. 
\v 2 And at that time \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd*, whose eyesight had grown so dim that he could not see, was lying in his room. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 Before the lamp of God had gone out, \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* was lying down in the temple of the LORD, where the ark of God was located. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Then the LORD called to \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*, and he answered, “Here I am.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 He ran to \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd* and said, “Here I am, for you have called me.” 
\b
\m “I did not call,” \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd* replied. “Go back and lie down.” 
\b
\m So he went and lay down. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 Once again the LORD called, “\zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*!” 
\b
\m So \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* got up, went to \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd*, and said, “Here I am, for you have called me.” 
\b
\m “\zwd My son|Q6577515\zwd*, I did not call,” \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd* replied. “Go back and lie down.” 
\b
\m 
\v 7 Now \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* did not yet know the LORD, because the word of the LORD had not yet been revealed to him. 
\v 8 Once again, for the third time, the LORD called to \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*. He got up, went to \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd*, and said, “Here I am, for you have called me.” 
\b
\m Then \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd* realized that it was the LORD who was calling \zwd the boy|Q6577515\zwd*. 
\v 9 “Go and lie down,” he said to \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*, “and if He calls you, say, ‘Speak, LORD, for \zwd Your servant|Q6577515\zwd* is listening.’” 
\b
\m So \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* went and lay down in his place. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Then the LORD came and stood there, calling as before, “\zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*! \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*!” 
\b
\m And \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* answered, “Speak, for \zwd Your servant|Q6577515\zwd* is listening.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 Then the LORD said to \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*, “I am about to do something in Israel at which the ears of all who hear it will tingle. 
\v 12 On that day I will carry out against \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd* everything I have spoken about his house, from beginning to end. 
\v 13 I told him that I would judge his house forever for the iniquity of which he knows, because \zwd his sons|Q1563312,Q20723908\zwd* blasphemed God \f + \fr 3:13 \ft LXX; Hebrew made themselves contemptible\f* and he did not restrain them. 
\v 14 Therefore I have sworn to the house of \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd*, ‘The iniquity of \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd*’s house shall never be atoned for by sacrifice or offering.’” 
\s1 Samuel Shares the Vision
\b
\m 
\v 15 \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* lay down until the morning; then he opened the doors of the house of the LORD. He was afraid to tell \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd* the vision, 
\v 16 but \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd* called to him and said, “\zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*, \zwd my son|Q6577515\zwd*.” 
\b
\m “Here I am,” answered \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 “What was the message He gave you?” \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd* asked. “Do not hide it from me. May God punish you, and ever so severely, if you hide from me anything He said to you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 So \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* told him everything and did not hide a thing from him. 
\b
\m “He is the LORD,” replied \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd*. “Let Him do what is good in His eyes.” 
\b
\m 
\v 19 And \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* grew, and the LORD was with him, and He let none of \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*’s words fall to the ground. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 So all Israel from Dan to Beersheba knew that \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* was confirmed as a prophet of the LORD. 
\v 21 And the LORD continued to appear at Shiloh, because there He revealed Himself to \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* by His word. 
\c 4
\s1 The Philistines Capture the Ark
\m 
\v 1 Thus the word of \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* came to all Israel. 
\b
\m Now the Israelites went out to meet the Philistines in battle and camped at Ebenezer, while the Philistines camped at Aphek. 
\v 2 The Philistines arrayed themselves against Israel, and as the battle spread, Israel was defeated by the Philistines, who struck down about four thousand men on the battlefield. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 When the troops returned to the camp, the elders of Israel asked, “Why has the LORD brought defeat on us before the Philistines today? Let us bring the ark of the covenant of the LORD from Shiloh, so that it may go \f + \fr 4:3 \ft Or He may go\f* with us to save us from the hand of our enemies.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 So the people sent men to Shiloh, and they brought back the ark of the covenant of the LORD of Hosts, who sits enthroned between the cherubim. And the \zwd two sons of \zwd Eli|id="Q362021" t="2"\zwd*|Q1563312,Q20723908\zwd*, \zwd Hophni|Q1563312\zwd* and \zwd Phinehas|Q20723908\zwd*, were there with the ark of the covenant of God. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 \zdate|Q362021:P570\*When the ark of the covenant of the LORD entered the camp, all the Israelites raised such a great shout that the ground shook. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 On hearing the noise of the shout, the Philistines asked, “What is this loud shouting in the camp of the Hebrews?” 
\b
\m And when they realized that the ark of the LORD had entered the camp, 
\v 7 the Philistines were afraid. “The gods have entered \f + \fr 4:7 \ft Or A god has entered\f* their camp!” they said. “Woe to us, for nothing like this has happened before. 
\v 8 Woe to us! Who will deliver us from the hand of these mighty gods? These are the gods who struck the Egyptians with all kinds of plagues in the wilderness. 
\v 9 Take courage and be men, O Philistines! Otherwise, you will serve the Hebrews just as they served you. Now be men and fight!” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 So the Philistines fought, and Israel was defeated, and each man fled to his tent. The slaughter was very great—thirty thousand foot soldiers of Israel fell. 
\v 11 The ark of God was captured, and \zwd \zwd Eli|id="Q362021" t="2"\zwd*’s two sons|Q1563312,Q20723908\zwd*, \zwd Hophni|Q1563312\zwd* and \zwd Phinehas|Q20723908\zwd*, died. 
\s1 The Death of Eli
\b
\m 
\v 12 That same day a Benjamite ran from the battle line all the way to Shiloh, with his clothes torn and dirt on his head. 
\v 13 When he arrived, there was \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd*, sitting on his chair beside the road and watching, because his heart trembled for the ark of God. 
\b
\m When the man entered the city to give a report, the whole city cried out. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd* heard the outcry and asked, “Why this commotion?” 
\b
\m So the man hurried over and reported to \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd*. 
\v 15 Now \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd* was ninety-eight years old, and his gaze was fixed because he could not see. 
\b
\m 
\v 16 “I have just come from the battle,” the man said to \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd*. “I fled from there today.” 
\b
\m “What happened, my son?” \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd* asked. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 The messenger answered, “Israel has fled before the Philistines, and there has been a great slaughter among the people. \zwd Your two sons|id="Q1563312,Q20723908" t="2"\zwd*, \zwd Hophni|id="Q1563312" t="2"\zwd* and \zwd Phinehas|id="Q20723908" t="2"\zwd*, are both dead, and the ark of God has been captured.” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 As soon as the ark of God was mentioned, \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd* fell backward from his chair by the city gate, and being old and heavy, he broke his neck and died. And \zwd Eli|Q362021\zwd* had judged \f + \fr 4:18 \ft Or governed or led\f* Israel forty years. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 Now \zwd \zwd Eli|id="Q362021" t="2"\zwd*’s daughter-in-law|Q7999473\zwd*, \zwd the wife of \zwd Phinehas|id="Q20723908" t="2"\zwd*|Q7999473\zwd*, was pregnant and about to give birth. When she heard the news of the capture of God’s ark and the deaths of \zwd her father-in-law|id="Q362021" t="2"\zwd* and \zwd her husband|id="Q20723908" t="2"\zwd*, she collapsed and gave birth, for her labor pains overtook her. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 As she was dying, the women attending to her said, “Do not be afraid, for you have given birth to \zwd a son|Q4205417\zwd*!” 
\b
\m But she did not respond or pay any heed. 
\v 21 And she named the boy \zwd Ichabod|Q4205417\zwd*,\f + \fr 4:21 \ft Ichabod means no glory.\f* saying, “The glory has departed \f + \fr 4:21 \ft Or gone into exile; also in verse 22\f* from Israel,” because the ark of God had been captured and \zwd her father-in-law|id="Q362021" t="2"\zwd* and \zwd her husband|id="Q20723908" t="2"\zwd* had been killed. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 “The glory has departed from Israel,” she said, “for the ark of God has been captured.”\zdate|Q362021:P570\* 
\c 5
\s1 The Ark Afflicts the Philistines
\m 
\v 1 After the Philistines had captured the ark of God, they took it from Ebenezer to Ashdod, 
\v 2 carried it into the temple of Dagon, and set it beside his statue.\f + \fr 5:2 \ft Literally set it beside Dagon\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 3 When the people of Ashdod got up early the next morning, there was Dagon, fallen on his face before the ark of the LORD. So they took Dagon and returned him to his place. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 But when they got up early the next morning, there was Dagon, fallen on his face before the ark of the LORD, with his head and his hands broken off and lying on the threshold. Only the torso remained. 
\v 5 That is why, to this day, the priests of Dagon and all who enter the temple of Dagon in Ashdod do not step on the threshold. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 Now the hand of the LORD was heavy on the people of Ashdod and its vicinity, ravaging them and afflicting them with tumors.\f + \fr 5:6 \ft Hebrew; LXX and Vulgate include And rats appeared in their land, and death and destruction were throughout the city.\f* 
\v 7 And when the men of Ashdod saw what was happening, they said, “The ark of the God of Israel must not stay here with us, because His hand is heavy upon us and upon our god Dagon.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 So they called together all the rulers of the Philistines and asked, “What shall we do with the ark of the God of Israel?” 
\b
\m “It must be moved to Gath,” they replied. So they carried away the ark of the God of Israel. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 But after they had moved the ark to Gath, the LORD’s hand was also against that city, throwing it into great confusion and afflicting the men of the city, both young and old, with an outbreak of tumors. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 So they sent the ark of God to Ekron, but as it arrived, the Ekronites cried out, “They have brought us the ark of the God of Israel in order to kill us and our people!” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 Then the Ekronites called together all the rulers of the Philistines and said, “Send away the ark of the God of Israel. It must return to its place, so that it will not \f + \fr 5:11 \ft Or He will not\f* kill us and our people!” 
\b
\m For a deadly confusion had pervaded the city; the hand of God was very heavy upon it. 
\v 12 Those who did not die were afflicted with tumors, and the outcry of the city went up to heaven. 
\c 6
\s1 The Ark Returned to Israel
\m 
\v 1 When the ark of the LORD had been in the land of the Philistines seven months, 
\v 2 the Philistines summoned the priests and diviners, saying, “What shall we do with the ark of the LORD? Tell us how to send it back to its place.” 
\b
\m 
\v 3 They replied, “If you return the ark of the God of Israel, do not send it away empty, but by all means return it to Him with a guilt offering. Then you will be healed, and you will understand why His hand has not been lifted from you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 “What guilt offering should we send back to Him?” asked the Philistines. 
\b
\m “Five gold tumors and five gold rats,” they said, “according to the number of rulers of the Philistines, since the same plague has struck both you and your rulers. 
\v 5 Make images of your tumors and of the rats that are ravaging the land. Give glory to the God of Israel, and perhaps He will lift His hand from you and your gods and your land. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 Why harden \f + \fr 6:6 \ft Or make heavy; similarly again in this verse\f* your hearts as the Egyptians and Pharaoh hardened theirs? When He afflicted them, did they not send the people out so they could go on their way? 
\b
\m 
\v 7 Now, therefore, prepare one new cart with two milk cows that have never been yoked. Hitch the cows to the cart, but take their calves away and pen them up. 
\v 8 Take the ark of the LORD, set it on the cart, and in a chest beside it put the gold objects you are sending back to Him as a guilt offering. 
\b
\m Then send the ark on its way, 
\v 9 but keep watching it. If it goes up the road to its homeland, toward Beth-shemesh, it is the LORD who has brought on us this great disaster. But if it does not, then we will know that it was not His hand that punished us and that it happened by chance.” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 So the men did as instructed. They took two milk cows, hitched them to the cart, and penned up their calves. 
\v 11 Then they put the ark of the LORD on the cart, along with the chest containing the gold rats and the images of the tumors. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 And the cows headed straight up the road toward Beth-shemesh, staying on that one highway and lowing as they went, never straying to the right or to the left. The rulers of the Philistines followed behind them to the border of Beth-shemesh. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 Now the people of Beth-shemesh were harvesting wheat in the valley, and when they looked up and saw the ark, they were overjoyed at the sight. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 The cart came to the field of \zwd Joshua of Beth-shemesh|\zwd* and stopped there near a large rock. The people chopped up the cart and offered the cows as a burnt offering to the LORD. 
\v 15 And the Levites took down the ark of the LORD and the chest containing the gold objects, and they placed them on the large rock. That day the men of Beth-shemesh offered burnt offerings and made sacrifices to the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 16 And when \zwd the five rulers of the Philistines|\zwd* saw this, they returned to Ekron that same day. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 As a guilt offering to the LORD, the Philistines had sent back one gold tumor for each city: Ashdod, Gaza, Ashkelon, Gath, and Ekron. 
\v 18 The number of gold rats also corresponded to the number of Philistine cities belonging to \zwd the five rulers|\zwd*—the fortified cities and their outlying villages. And the large rock \f + \fr 6:18 \ft Or great meadow; Hebrew Abel-haggedolah\f* on which they placed the ark of the LORD stands to this day in the field of \zwd Joshua of Beth-shemesh|\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 But God struck down some of the people of Beth-shemesh because they looked inside the ark of the LORD. He struck down seventy men,\f + \fr 6:19 \ft A few late Hebrew manuscripts and Josephus; most Hebrew manuscripts 70 men and 50,000 men; LXX 70 men and 50,000 men of the people; Syriac and Arabic 70 men and 5,000 men; alternately, possibly 70 men and 50 oxen\f* and the people mourned because the LORD had struck them with a great slaughter. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 The men of Beth-shemesh asked, “Who can stand in the presence of the LORD, this holy God? To whom should the ark go up from here?” 
\b
\m 
\v 21 So they sent messengers to the people of Kiriath-jearim, saying, “The Philistines have returned the ark of the LORD. Come down and take it up with you.” 
\c 7
\s1 Samuel Subdues the Philistines
\m 
\v 1 Then the men of Kiriath-jearim came for the ark of the LORD and took it into \zwd Abinadab|Q124290973\zwd*’s house on the hill. And they consecrated \zwd his son Eleazar|Q5354448\zwd* to guard the ark of the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 2 And from that day a long time passed, twenty years in all, as the ark remained at Kiriath-jearim. And all the house of Israel mournfully sought the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 Then \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* said to all the house of Israel, “If you are returning to the LORD with all your hearts, then put away the foreign gods and Ashtoreths among you, prepare your hearts for the LORD, and serve Him only. And He will deliver you from the hand of the Philistines.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 So the Israelites put away the Baals and Ashtoreths and served only the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 Then \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* said, “Gather all Israel to Mizpah, and I will pray to the LORD on your behalf.” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 When they had gathered at Mizpah, they drew water and poured it out before the LORD. On that day they fasted, and there they confessed, “We have sinned against the LORD.” And \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* judged \f + \fr 7:6 \ft Or governed or led; similarly in verses 15, 16, and 17\f* the Israelites at Mizpah. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 When the Philistines heard that the Israelites had gathered at Mizpah, their rulers marched up toward Israel. And when the Israelites learned of this, they feared the Philistines 
\v 8 and said to \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*, “Do not stop crying out to the LORD our God for us, that He may save us from the hand of the Philistines.” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 Then \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* took a suckling lamb and offered it as a whole burnt offering to the LORD. He cried out to the LORD on behalf of Israel, and the LORD answered him. 
\v 10 As the Philistines drew near to fight against Israel, \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* was offering up the burnt offering. But that day the LORD thundered loudly against the Philistines and threw them into such confusion that they fled before Israel. 
\b
\m 
\v 11 Then the men of Israel charged out of Mizpah and pursued the Philistines, striking them down all the way to an area below Beth-car. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 Afterward, \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* took a stone and set it up between Mizpah and Shen.\f + \fr 7:12 \ft Hebrew; LXX and Syriac Jeshanah\f* He named it Ebenezer,\f + \fr 7:12 \ft Ebenezer means stone of help.\f* saying, “Thus far the LORD has helped us.” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 So the Philistines were subdued, and they stopped invading the territory of Israel. And the hand of the LORD was against the Philistines all the days of \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*. 
\v 14 The cities from Ekron to Gath, which the Philistines had taken, were restored to Israel, who also delivered the surrounding territory from the hand of the Philistines. And there was peace between the Israelites and the Amorites. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 So \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* judged Israel all the days of his life. 
\v 16 Every year he would go on a circuit from Bethel to Gilgal to Mizpah, judging Israel in all these places. 
\v 17 Then he would return to Ramah because his home was there, and there he judged Israel and built an altar to the LORD. 
\c 8
\s1 Israel Demands a King
\r (Deuteronomy 17:14–20)
\m 
\v 1 When \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* grew old, he appointed \zwd his sons|Q20888416,Q3603527\zwd* as judges \f + \fr 8:1 \ft Or governors or leaders; similarly in verses 2, 5, 6, and 20\f* over Israel. 
\v 2 The name of \zwd his firstborn son|Q20888416\zwd* was \zwd Joel|Q20888416\zwd*, and the name of his second was \zwd Abijah|Q3603527\zwd*. They were judges in Beersheba. 
\v 3 But \zwd his sons|Q20888416,Q3603527\zwd* did not walk in his ways; they turned aside toward dishonest gain, accepting bribes and perverting justice. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 So all the elders of Israel gathered together and came to \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* at Ramah. 
\v 5 “Look,” they said, “you are old, and \zwd your sons|Q20888416,Q3603527\zwd* do not walk in your ways. Now appoint a king to judge us like all the other nations.” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 But when they said, “Give us a king to judge us,” their demand was displeasing in the sight of \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*; so he prayed to the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 And the LORD said to \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*, “Listen to the voice of the people in all that they say to you. For it is not you they have rejected, but they have rejected Me as their king. 
\v 8 Just as they have done from the day I brought them up out of Egypt until this day, forsaking Me and serving other gods, so they are doing to you. 
\v 9 Now listen to their voice; but you must solemnly warn them and show them the manner of the king who will reign over them.” 
\s1 Samuel’s Warning
\b
\m 
\v 10 So \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* spoke all the words of the LORD to the people who were asking him for a king. 
\v 11 He said, “This will be the manner of the king who will reign over you: He will take your sons and appoint them to serve his own chariots and horses, and to run in front of his chariots. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 He will appoint some for himself as commanders of thousands and of fifties, and others to plow his ground, to reap his harvest, and to make his weapons of war and equipment for his chariots. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 And he will take your daughters to be perfumers, cooks, and bakers. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 He will take the best of your fields and vineyards and olive groves and give them to his servants. 
\v 15 He will take a tenth of your grain and grape harvest and give it to his officials and servants. 
\v 16 And he will take your menservants and maidservants and your best cattle \f + \fr 8:16 \ft LXX; Hebrew your best young men\f* and donkeys and put them to his own use. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 He will take a tenth of your flocks, and you yourselves will become his slaves. 
\v 18 When that day comes, you will beg for relief from the king you have chosen, but the LORD will not answer you on that day.” 
\s1 God Grants the Request
\b
\m 
\v 19 Nevertheless, the people refused to listen to \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*. “No!” they said. “We must have a king over us. 
\v 20 Then we will be like all the other nations, with a king to judge us, to go out before us, and to fight our battles.” 
\b
\m 
\v 21 \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* listened to all the words of the people and repeated them in the hearing of the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 “Listen to their voice,” the LORD said to \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*. “Appoint a king for them.” 
\b
\m Then \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* told the men of Israel, “Everyone must go back to his city.” 
\c 9
\s1 Saul Chosen as King
\m
\v 1 Now there was a Benjamite, a powerful man, whose name was \zwd Kish son of \zwd Abiel|id="Q16128781" t="2"\zwd*|Q2915270\zwd*, the son of \zwd Zeror|id="Q3541493" t="2"\zwd*, the son of \zwd Becorath|id="Q2929707" t="2"\zwd*, the son of \zwd Aphiah of Benjamin|id="Q2858222" t="2"\zwd*.
\v 2 And he had \zwd a son|Q28730\zwd* named \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, choice and handsome, without equal among the Israelites—a head taller than any of the people.
\b
\m
\v 3 One day the donkeys of \zwd \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*’s father Kish|Q2915270\zwd* wandered off, and \zwd Kish|Q2915270\zwd* said to \zwd his son Saul|Q28730\zwd*, “Take one of the servants and go look for the donkeys.”
\b
\m
\v 4 So \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* passed through the hill country of Ephraim and then through the land of Shalishah, but they did not find the donkeys. He and the servant went through the region of Shaalim, but they were not there. Then they went through the land of Benjamin, and still they did not find them.
\b
\m
\v 5 When they reached the land of Zuph, \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* said to his servant, “Come, let us go back, or \zwd my father|id="Q2915270" t="2"\zwd* will stop worrying about the donkeys and start worrying about us.”
\b
\m
\v 6 “Look,” said the servant, “in this city there is \zwd a man of God|Q6577515\zwd* who is highly respected; everything he says surely comes to pass. Let us go there now. Perhaps he will tell us which way to go.”
\b
\m
\v 7 “If we do go,” \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* replied, “what can we give \zwd the man|Q6577515\zwd*? For the bread in our packs is gone, and there is no gift to take to the man of God. What do we have?”
\b
\m
\v 8 The servant answered him again. “Look,” he said, “I have here in my hand a quarter shekel of silver.\f + \fr 9:8 \ft A quarter shekel is approximately 0.1 ounces or 2.85 grams of silver.\f* I will give it to \zwd the man of God|Q6577515\zwd*, and he will tell us our way.”
\b
\m
\v 9 (Formerly in Israel, a man on his way to inquire of God would say, “Come, let us go to \zwd the seer|Q6577515\zwd*.” For the prophet of today was formerly called \zwd the seer|Q6577515\zwd*.)
\b
\m
\v 10 “Good,” said \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* to his servant. “Come, let us go.” So they set out for the city where \zwd the man of God|Q6577515\zwd* was.
\v 11 And as they were climbing the hill to the city, they met some young women coming out to draw water and asked, “Is \zwd the seer|Q6577515\zwd* here?”
\b
\m
\v 12 “Yes, he is ahead of you,” they answered. “Hurry now, for today he has come to the city because the people have a sacrifice on the high place.
\v 13 As soon as you enter the city, you will find him before he goes up to the high place to eat. The people will not eat until he comes, because he must bless the sacrifice; after that, the guests will eat. Go up at once; you will find him.”
\b
\m
\v 14 So \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* and his servant went up toward the city, and as they were entering it, there was \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* coming toward them on his way up to the high place.
\b
\m
\v 15 Now on the day before \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*’s arrival, the LORD had revealed to \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*,
\v 16 “At this time tomorrow I will send you \zwd a man from the land of Benjamin|Q28730\zwd*, and you are to anoint him ruler over My people Israel; he will save them from the hand of the Philistines. For I have looked upon My people, because their cry has come to Me.”
\b
\m
\v 17 When \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* saw \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, the LORD told him, “Here is the man of whom I spoke; he shall rule over My people.”
\b
\m
\v 18 \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* approached \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* in the gateway and asked, “Would you please tell me where \zwd the seer|Q6577515\zwd*’s house is?”
\b
\m
\v 19 “I am \zwd the seer|Q6577515\zwd*,” \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* replied. “Go up before me to the high place, for you shall eat with me today. And when I send you off in the morning, I will tell you all that is in your heart.
\v 20 As for the donkeys you lost three days ago, do not worry about them, for they have been found. And upon whom is all the desire of Israel, if not upon you and all \zwd your father|id="Q2915270" t="2"\zwd*’s house?”
\b
\m
\v 21 \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* replied, “Am I not a Benjamite from the smallest tribe of Israel, and is not my clan the least of all the clans of Benjamin? So why would you say such a thing to me?”
\b
\m
\v 22 Then \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* took \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* and his servant, brought them into the hall, and seated them in the place of honor among those who were invited—about thirty in all.
\v 23 And \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* said to the cook, “Bring the portion I gave you and told you to set aside.”
\b
\m
\v 24 So the cook picked up the leg and what was attached to it and set it before \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*. Then \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* said, “Here is what was kept back. It was set apart for you. Eat, for it has been kept for you for this occasion, from the time I said, ‘I have invited the people.’” So \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* dined with \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* that day.
\b
\m
\v 25 And after they had come down from the high place into the city, \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* spoke with \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* on the roof of his house.
\b
\m
\v 26 \zdate|Q28730:P39:Q43459871:P580\*They got up early in the morning, and just before dawn \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* called to \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* on the roof, “Get ready, and I will send you on your way!” So \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* got ready, and both he and \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* went outside together.
\b
\m
\v 27 As they were going down to the edge of the city, \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* said to \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, “Tell the servant to go on ahead of us, but you stay for a while, and I will reveal to you the word of God.” So the servant went on.
\c 10
\s1 Samuel Anoints Saul
\m 
\v 1 Then \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* took a flask of oil, poured it on \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*’s head, kissed him, and said, “Has not the LORD anointed you ruler over His inheritance?\f + \fr 10:1 \ft Hebrew; LXX “Has not the LORD anointed you ruler over Israel? And you will rule over the LORD’s people and save them from their enemies around them. This will be the sign to you that the LORD has appointed you to be leader over His inheritance.\f* 
\v 2 When you leave me today, you will find two men at \zwd Rachel|id="Q207389" t="2"\zwd*’s tomb in Zelzah on the border of Benjamin. They will say to you, ‘The donkeys you seek have been found, and now \zwd your father|id="Q2915270" t="2"\zwd* has stopped worrying about the donkeys and started worrying about you, asking, “What should I do about \zwd my son|Q28730\zwd*?”’ 
\b
\m 
\v 3 Then you will go on from there until you come to the Oak \f + \fr 10:3 \ft Or Terebinth or Great Tree\f* of Tabor. Three men going up to God at Bethel will meet you there, one carrying three young goats, another carrying three loaves of bread, and another carrying a skin of wine. 
\v 4 They will greet you and give you two loaves of bread, which you will accept from their hands. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 After that you will come to Gibeah of God,\f + \fr 10:5 \ft Hebrew Gibeath-Elohim, meaning the hill of God\f* where the Philistines have an outpost. As you approach the city, you will meet a group of prophets coming down from the high place, preceded by harps, tambourines, flutes, and lyres, and they will be prophesying. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 Then the Spirit of the LORD will rush upon you, and you will prophesy with them; and you will be transformed into a different person. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 When these signs have come, do as the occasion demands, for God is with you. 
\v 8 And you shall go before me to Gilgal, and surely I will come to you to offer burnt offerings and to sacrifice peace offerings. Wait seven days until I come to you and show you what you are to do.” 
\s1 Samuel’s Signs Fulfilled
\b
\m 
\v 9 As \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* turned to leave \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*, God changed \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*’s heart, and all the signs came to pass that day. 
\v 10 When \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* and his servant arrived at Gibeah,\f + \fr 10:10 \ft Gibeah means the hill.\f* a group of prophets met him. Then the Spirit of God rushed upon him, and he prophesied along with them. 
\b
\m 
\v 11 When all those who had formerly known \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* saw him prophesying with the prophets, they asked one another, “What has happened to the son of \zwd Kish|id="Q2915270" t="2"\zwd*? Is \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* also among the prophets?” 
\b
\m 
\v 12 Then a man who lived there replied, “And who is their father?” So the saying became a proverb: “Is \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* also among the prophets?” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 And when \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* had finished prophesying, he went up to the high place. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 Now \zwd \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*’s uncle|\zwd* asked him and his servant, “Where did you go?” 
\b
\m “To look for the donkeys,” \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* replied. “When we saw they were not to be found, we went to \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 15 “Tell me,” \zwd \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*’s uncle|\zwd* asked, “what did \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* say to you?” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 And \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* replied, “He assured us that the donkeys had been found.” But \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* did not tell \zwd his uncle|\zwd* what \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* had said about the kingship. 
\s1 Saul Proclaimed King
\b
\m 
\v 17 After this, \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* summoned the people to the LORD at Mizpah 
\v 18 and said to the Israelites, “This is what the LORD, the God of Israel, says: ‘I brought Israel up out of Egypt, and I rescued you from the hands of the Egyptians and of all the kingdoms that oppressed you.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 19 But today you have rejected your God, who saves you from all your troubles and afflictions, and you have said to Him, ‘No, set a king over us.’ Now therefore present yourselves before the LORD by your tribes and clans.” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 Thus \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* had all the tribes of Israel come forward, and the tribe of Benjamin was selected. 
\v 21 Then he had the tribe of Benjamin come forward by its clans, and the clan of \zwd Matri|\zwd* was selected.\f + \fr 10:21 \ft LXX includes And he brought the family of the Matrites near, man by man.\f* Finally, \zwd Saul son of \zwd Kish|id="Q2915270" t="2"\zwd*|Q28730\zwd* was selected. But when they looked for him, they could not find him. 
\v 22 So again they inquired of the LORD, “Has the man come here yet?” 
\b
\m And the LORD replied, “Behold, he has hidden himself among the baggage.” 
\b
\m 
\v 23 So they ran and brought \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, and when he stood among the people, he was a head taller than any of the others. 
\v 24 \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* said to all the people, “Do you see the one the LORD has chosen? There is no one like him among all the people.” 
\b
\m And all the people shouted, “Long live \zwd the king|Q28730\zwd*!” 
\b
\m 
\v 25 Then \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* explained to the people the rights of kingship. He wrote them on a scroll and laid it up before the LORD. And \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* sent all the people away, each to his own home. 
\b
\m 
\v 26 \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* also went to his home in Gibeah, and the men of valor whose hearts God had touched went with him. 
\b
\m 
\v 27 But some worthless men said, “How can this man save us?” So they despised him and brought him no gifts; but \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* remained silent about it.\f + \fr 10:27 \ft MT and LXX; one DSS manuscript includes Nahash, king of the Ammonites, had viciously oppressed the people of Gad and Reuben, gouging out the right eye of each Israelite dwelling there. He would not allow anyone to rescue them, and there was no Israelite east of the Jordan whose right eye had not been gouged out. But 7,000 men had escaped from the Ammonites and settled in Jabesh-gilead.\f*\zdate|Q28730:P39:Q43459871:P580\* 
\c 11
\s1 Saul Defeats the Ammonites
\m 
\v 1 Then \zwd Nahash \f + \fr 11:1 \ft DSS and LXX About a month later Nahash\f* the Ammonite|Q6959218\zwd* came up and laid siege to Jabesh-gilead. All the men of Jabesh said to him, “Make a treaty \f + \fr 11:1 \ft Forms of the Hebrew berit are translated in most passages as covenant.\f* with us, and we will serve you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 2 But \zwd Nahash the Ammonite|Q6959218\zwd* replied, “I will make a treaty with you on one condition, that I may put out everyone’s right eye and bring reproach upon all Israel.” 
\b
\m 
\v 3 “Hold off for seven days,” replied the elders of Jabesh, “and let us send messengers throughout Israel. If there is no one to save us, we will surrender to you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 When the messengers came to Gibeah of \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* and relayed these words in the hearing of the people, they all wept aloud. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 Just then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* was returning from the field, behind his oxen. “What troubles the people?” asked \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*. “Why are they weeping?” And they relayed to him the words of the men from Jabesh. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 When \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* heard their words, the Spirit of God rushed upon him, and he burned with great anger. 
\v 7 He took a pair of oxen, cut them into pieces, and sent them by messengers throughout the land of Israel, proclaiming, “This is what will be done to the oxen of anyone who does not march behind \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* and \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*.” 
\b
\m Then the terror of the LORD fell upon the people, and they came out together as one man. 
\v 8 And when \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* numbered them at Bezek, there were 300,000 Israelites and 30,000 \f + \fr 11:8 \ft DSS and LXX 70,000\f* men of Judah. 
\v 9 So they said to the messengers who had come, “Tell the men of Jabesh-gilead: ‘Deliverance will be yours tomorrow by the time the sun is hot.’” 
\b
\m And when the messengers relayed this to the men of Jabesh, they rejoiced. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Then the men of Jabesh said to \zwd Nahash|Q6959218\zwd*, “Tomorrow we will come out, and you can do with us whatever seems good to you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 The next day \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* organized the troops into three divisions, and during the morning watch they invaded the camp of the Ammonites and slaughtered them, until the hottest part of the day. And the survivors were so scattered that no two of them were left together. 
\s1 Saul Confirmed as King
\b
\m 
\v 12 Then the people said to \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*, “Who said that \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* should not reign over us? Bring those men here so we can kill them!” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 But \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* ordered, “No one shall be put to death this day, for today the LORD has worked salvation in Israel.” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 Then \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* said to the people, “Come, let us go to Gilgal and renew the kingship there.” 
\b
\m 
\v 15 So all the people went to Gilgal and confirmed \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* as king in the presence of the LORD. There they sacrificed peace offerings before the LORD, and \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* and all the Israelites rejoiced greatly. 
\c 12
\s1 Samuel’s Farewell Address
\m 
\v 1 Then \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* said to all Israel, “I have listened to your voice in all that you have said to me, and I have set over you a king. 
\v 2 Now here is \zwd the king|Q28730\zwd* walking before you, and I am old and gray, and \zwd my sons|Q20888416,Q3603527\zwd* are here with you. I have walked before you from my youth until this day. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 Here I am. Bear witness against me before the LORD and before His anointed: Whose ox or donkey have I taken? Whom have I cheated or oppressed? From whose hand have I accepted a bribe and closed my eyes? Tell me, and I will restore it \f + \fr 12:3 \ft Hebrew And I will restore it; LXX Testify against me, and I will restore it\f* to you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 “You have not cheated us or oppressed us,” they replied, “nor have you taken anything from the hand of man.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* said to them, “The LORD is a witness against you, and His anointed is a witness today, that you have not found anything in my hand.” 
\b
\m “He is a witness,” they replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 Then \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* said to the people, “The LORD is the One who \f + \fr 12:6 \ft Hebrew; LXX The LORD is the witness who\f* appointed \zwd Moses|id="Q9077" t="2"\zwd* and \zwd Aaron|id="Q51676" t="2"\zwd*, and who brought your fathers up out of the land of Egypt. 
\v 7 Now present yourselves, so that I may confront you before the LORD with all the righteous acts He has done for you and your fathers. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 When \zwd Jacob|id="Q289957" t="2"\zwd* went to Egypt,\f + \fr 12:8 \ft Hebrew; LXX includes and the Egyptians oppressed them\f* your fathers cried out to the LORD, and He sent them \zwd Moses|id="Q9077" t="2"\zwd* and \zwd Aaron|id="Q51676" t="2"\zwd*, who brought your fathers out of Egypt and settled them in this place. 
\v 9 But they forgot the LORD their God, and He sold them into the hand of \zwd Sisera the commander of the army of Hazor|Q976765\zwd*,\f + \fr 12:9 \ft LXX the army of Jabin king of Hazor\f* and into the hands of the Philistines and \zwd the king of Moab|\zwd*, who fought against them. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Then they cried out to the LORD and said, ‘We have sinned, for we have forsaken the LORD and served the Baals and Ashtoreths. Now deliver us from the hands of our enemies, that we may serve You.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 11 So the LORD sent \zwd Jerubbaal|id="Q176125" t="2"\zwd*,\f + \fr 12:11 \ft Jerubbaal is another name for Gideon and probably means let Baal contend; see Judges 6:32.\f* \zwd Barak|id="Q807236" t="2"\zwd*,\f + \fr 12:11 \ft LXX and Syriac; Hebrew Bedan\f* \zwd Jephthah|id="Q1133523" t="2"\zwd*, and \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*,\f + \fr 12:11 \ft LXX and Syriac Samson\f* and He delivered you from the hands of your enemies on every side, and you dwelt securely. 
\v 12 But when you saw that \zwd Nahash king of the Ammonites|id="Q6959218" t="2"\zwd* was moving against you, you said to me, ‘No, we must have a king to rule over us’—even though the LORD your God was your king. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 Now here is \zwd the king you have chosen|Q28730\zwd*, the one you requested. Behold, the LORD has placed a king over you. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 If you fear the LORD and serve Him and obey His voice, and if you do not rebel against the command of the LORD, and if both you and the king who rules over you follow the LORD your God, then all will be well.\f + \fr 12:14 \ft then all will be well is implied; Literally If you fear the LORD and serve Him and obey His voice, and if you do not rebel against the command of the LORD, (then) both you and the king who rules over you will follow the LORD your God.\f* 
\v 15 But if you disobey the LORD and rebel against His command, then the hand of the LORD will be against you as it was against your fathers.\f + \fr 12:15 \ft Hebrew; LXX against your king\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 16 Now, therefore, present yourselves and see this great thing that the LORD will do before your eyes. 
\v 17 Is it not the wheat harvest today? I will call on the LORD to send thunder and rain, so that you will know and see what a great evil you have committed in the sight of the LORD by asking for a king.” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 So \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* called to the LORD, and on that day the LORD sent thunder and rain. 
\b
\m As a result, all the people greatly feared the LORD and \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*. 
\v 19 They pleaded with \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*, “Pray to the LORD your God for your servants so that we will not die! For we have added to all our sins the evil of asking for a king.” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 “Do not be afraid,” \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* replied. “Even though you have committed all this evil, do not turn aside from following the LORD, but serve the LORD with all your heart. 
\v 21 Do not turn aside after worthless things that cannot profit you or deliver you, for they are empty. 
\v 22 Indeed, for the sake of His great name, the LORD will not abandon His people, because He was pleased to make you His own. 
\b
\m 
\v 23 As for me, far be it from me that I should sin against the LORD by ceasing to pray for you. And I will continue to teach you the good and right way. 
\b
\m 
\v 24 Above all, fear the LORD and serve Him faithfully with all your heart; consider what great things He has done for you. 
\v 25 But if you persist in doing evil, both you and your king will be swept away.” 
\c 13
\s1 War with the Philistines
\m 
\v 1 \zdate|Q28730:P39:Q43459871:P580\*\zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* was thirty years old\f + \fr 13:1 \ft A few late LXX manuscripts; MT Saul was a son of a year\f* when he became king, and he reigned over Israel forty-two years.\f + \fr 13:1 \ft Or over Israel forty years (see Acts 13:21); MT over Israel two years\f* 
\v 2 He chose for himself three thousand men of Israel: Two thousand were with \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* at Michmash and in the hill country of Bethel, and a thousand were with \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* in Gibeah of Benjamin. And the rest of the troops he sent away, each to his own home. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 Then \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* attacked the Philistine outpost at Geba, and the Philistines heard about it. So \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* blew the ram’s horn throughout the land, saying, “Let the Hebrews hear!” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 And all Israel heard the news: “\zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* has attacked an outpost of the Philistines, and now Israel has become a stench to the Philistines!” Then the people were summoned to join \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* at Gilgal. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 Now the Philistines assembled to fight against Israel with three thousand \f + \fr 13:5 \ft Some LXX manuscripts and Syriac; Hebrew thirty thousand\f* chariots, six thousand horsemen, and troops as numerous as the sand on the seashore. They went up and camped at Michmash, east of Beth-aven. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 Seeing that they were in danger because their troops were hard-pressed, the men of Israel hid in caves and thickets, among the rocks, and in cellars and cisterns. 
\v 7 Some Hebrews even crossed the Jordan into the land of Gad and Gilead. \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, however, remained at Gilgal, and all his troops were quaking in fear. 
\s1 Saul’s Unlawful Sacrifice
\b
\m 
\v 8 And \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* waited seven days for the time appointed by \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*, but \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* did not come to Gilgal, and the troops began to desert \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*. 
\v 9 So he said, “Bring me the burnt offering and the peace offerings.” And he offered up the burnt offering. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Just as he finished offering the burnt offering, \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* arrived, and \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* went out to greet him. 
\b
\m 
\v 11 “What have you done?” \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* asked. 
\b
\m And \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* replied, “When I saw that the troops were deserting me, and that you did not come at the appointed time and the Philistines were gathering at Michmash, 
\v 12 I thought, ‘Now the Philistines will descend upon me at Gilgal, and I have not sought the favor of the LORD.’ So I felt compelled to offer the burnt offering.” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 “You have acted foolishly,” \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* declared. “You have not kept the command that the LORD your God gave you; if you had, the LORD would have established your kingdom over Israel for all time. 
\v 14 But now your kingdom will not endure; the LORD has sought a man after His own heart and appointed him ruler over His people, because you have not kept the command of the LORD.” 
\b
\m 
\v 15 Then \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* set out from Gilgal and went up to Gibeah in Benjamin.\f + \fr 13:15 \ft LXX Then Samuel set out, and the rest of the people went up after \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* to meet the army; they went up from Gilgal to Gibeah of Benjamin.\f* And \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* numbered the troops who were with him, about six hundred men. 
\s1 Israel without Weapons
\b
\m 
\v 16 Now \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* and \zwd Jonathan his son|Q74891\zwd* and the troops with them were staying in Geba of Benjamin, while the Philistines camped at Michmash. 
\v 17 And raiders went out of the Philistine camp in three divisions. One headed toward Ophrah in the land of Shual, 
\v 18 another toward Beth-horon, and the third down the border road overlooking the Valley of Zeboim facing the wilderness. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 And no blacksmith could be found in all the land of Israel, because the Philistines had said, “The Hebrews must not be allowed to make swords or spears.” 
\v 20 Instead, all the Israelites would go down to the Philistines to sharpen their plowshares, mattocks, axes, and sickles.\f + \fr 13:20 \ft LXX; Hebrew and plowshares; (so plowshare appears twice in the Hebrew).\f* 
\v 21 The charge was a pim \f + \fr 13:21 \ft A pim possibly refers to a polished stone weighing approximately 0.25 ounces or 7 grams found in excavations. This is equivalent to about two-thirds of a shekel and likely refers to the price charged by the Philistines for the services listed.\f* for sharpening a plowshare or mattock, a third of a shekel for sharpening a pitchfork or an axe, and a third of a shekel for repointing an oxgoad.\f + \fr 13:21 \ft Hebrew does not include the currency unit of a shekel charged for sharpening a pitchfork, an axe, or an oxgoad; alternatively, possibly a third of a pim for each.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 22 So on the day of battle not a sword or spear could be found in the hands of the troops with \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* and \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd*; only \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* and \zwd his son Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* had weapons. 
\b
\m 
\v 23 And a garrison of the Philistines had gone out to the pass at Michmash. 
\c 14
\s1 Jonathan’s Victory over the Philistines
\m 
\v 1 One day \zwd Jonathan son of \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*|Q74891\zwd* said to the young man bearing his armor, “Come, let us cross over to the Philistine outpost on the other side.” But \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* did not tell \zwd his father|Q28730\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 2 Meanwhile, \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* was staying under the pomegranate tree \f + \fr 14:2 \ft Or around the rock of Rimmon or in the pomegranate cave; see Judges 20:45, Judges 20:47, and Judges 21:13.\f* in Migron on the outskirts of Gibeah. And the troops who were with him numbered about six hundred men, 
\v 3 including \zwd Ahijah|\zwd*, who was wearing an ephod. He was the son of \zwd \zwd Ichabod|id="Q4205417" t="2"\zwd*’s brother Ahitub son of \zwd Phinehas|id="Q20723908" t="2"\zwd*|id="Q4695144" t="2"\zwd*, the son of \zwd Eli the priest|id="Q362021" t="2"\zwd* of the LORD in Shiloh. But the troops did not know that \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* had left. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Now there were cliffs on both sides of the pass that \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* intended to cross to reach the Philistine outpost. One was named Bozez and the other Seneh. 
\v 5 One cliff stood to the north toward Michmash, and the other to the south toward Geba. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* said to the young man bearing his armor, “Come, let us cross over to the outpost of these uncircumcised men. Perhaps the LORD will work on our behalf. Nothing can hinder the LORD from saving, whether by many or by few.” 
\b
\m 
\v 7 His armor-bearer replied, “Do all that is in your heart. Go ahead; I am with you heart and soul.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 “Very well,” said \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd*, “we will cross over toward these men and show ourselves to them. 
\v 9 If they say, ‘Wait until we come to you,’ then we will stay where we are and will not go up to them. 
\v 10 But if they say, ‘Come on up,’ then we will go up, because this will be our sign that the LORD has delivered them into our hands.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 So the two of them showed themselves to the outpost of the Philistines, who exclaimed, “Look, the Hebrews are coming out of the holes in which they were hiding!” 
\b
\m 
\v 12 So the men of the outpost called out to \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* and his armor-bearer, “Come on up, and we will teach you a lesson!” 
\b
\m “Follow me,” \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* told his armor-bearer, “for the LORD has delivered them into the hand of Israel.” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 So \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* climbed up on his hands and feet, with his armor-bearer behind him. And the Philistines fell before \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd*, and his armor-bearer followed and finished them off. 
\v 14 In that first assault, \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* and his armor-bearer struck down about twenty men in about half an acre \f + \fr 14:14 \ft Hebrew half a yoke. A yoke was the amount of land plowed by a pair of yoked oxen in one day.\f* of land. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 Then panic struck the Philistines in the camp, in the field, and among all the people. Even those in the outposts and raiding parties trembled. Indeed, the earth quaked, and panic spread from God.\f + \fr 14:15 \ft Or and a terrible panic spread\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 16 Now when \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*’s watchmen at Gibeah in Benjamin looked and saw the troops melting away and scattering in every direction,\f + \fr 14:16 \ft Or melting away and going here and there\f* 
\v 17 \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* said to the troops who were with him, “Call the roll and see who has left us.” 
\b
\m And when they had called the roll, they saw that \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* and his armor-bearer were not there. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* said to \zwd Ahijah|\zwd*, “Bring the ark of God.” (For at that time it was with the Israelites.)\f + \fr 14:18 \ft Hebrew; LXX “Bring the ephod.” For at that time he wore the ephod before the Israelites.\f* 
\v 19 While \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* was talking to the priest, the commotion in the Philistine camp continued to increase. So \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* said to the priest, “Withdraw your hand.” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* and all his troops assembled and marched to the battle, and they found the Philistines in total confusion, with each man wielding the sword against his neighbor. 
\v 21 And the Hebrews who had previously gone up into the surrounding camps to join the Philistines now went over to the Israelites who were with \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* and \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd*. 
\v 22 When all the Israelites who had been hiding in the hill country of Ephraim heard that the Philistines were fleeing, they also joined the battle in close pursuit. 
\b
\m 
\v 23 So the LORD saved Israel that day, and the battle moved on beyond Beth-aven. 
\s1 Jonathan Eats the Honey
\b
\m 
\v 24 Now the men of Israel were in distress that day, for \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* had placed the troops under an oath, saying, “Cursed is the man who eats any food before evening, before I have taken vengeance on my enemies.” So none of the troops tasted any food. 
\b
\m 
\v 25 Then all the troops entered the forest, and there was honey on the ground. 
\v 26 And when they entered the forest and saw the flowing honey, not one of them put his hand to his mouth, because they feared the oath. 
\b
\m 
\v 27 \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd*, however, had not heard that \zwd his father|Q28730\zwd* had bound the people with the oath. So he reached out the end of the staff in his hand, dipped it into the honeycomb, and put his hand to his mouth, and his eyes brightened.\f + \fr 14:27 \ft Or his strength was renewed; similarly in verse 29\f* 
\v 28 Then one of the soldiers told him, “\zwd Your father|Q28730\zwd* bound the troops with a solemn oath, saying, ‘Cursed is the man who eats food today.’ That is why the people are faint.” 
\b
\m 
\v 29 “\zwd My father|Q28730\zwd* has brought trouble to the land,” \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* replied. “Just look at how my eyes have brightened because I tasted a little of this honey. 
\v 30 How much better it would have been if the troops had eaten freely today from the plunder they took from their enemies! Would not the slaughter of the Philistines have been much greater?” 
\b
\m 
\v 31 That day, after the Israelites had struck down the Philistines from Michmash to Aijalon, the people were very faint. 
\v 32 So they rushed greedily to the plunder, taking sheep, cattle, and calves. They slaughtered them on the ground and ate meat with the blood still in it. 
\b
\m 
\v 33 Then someone reported to \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*: “Look, the troops are sinning against the LORD by eating meat with the blood still in it.” 
\b
\m “You have broken faith,” said \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*. “Roll a large stone over here at once.” 
\v 34 Then he said, “Go among the troops and tell them, ‘Each man must bring me his ox or his sheep, slaughter them in this place, and then eat. Do not sin against the LORD by eating meat with the blood still in it.’” 
\b
\m So that night everyone brought his ox and slaughtered it there. 
\v 35 Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* built an altar to the LORD; it was the first time he had built an altar to the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 36 And \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* said, “Let us go down after the Philistines by night and plunder them until dawn, leaving no man alive!” 
\b
\m “Do what seems good to you,” the troops replied. 
\b
\m But the priest said, “We must consult God here.” 
\s1 The People Save Jonathan
\b
\m 
\v 37 So \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* inquired of God, “Shall I go down after the Philistines? Will You give them into the hand of Israel?” 
\b
\m But God did not answer him that day. 
\b
\m 
\v 38 Therefore \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* said, “Come here, all you leaders of the troops, and let us investigate how this sin has occurred today. 
\v 39 As surely as the LORD who saves Israel lives, even if it is \zwd my son Jonathan|Q74891\zwd*, he must die!” 
\b
\m But not one of the troops said a word. 
\b
\m 
\v 40 Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* said to all Israel, “You stand on one side, and I and \zwd my son Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* will stand on the other side.” 
\b
\m “Do what seems good to you,” the troops replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 41 So \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* said to the LORD, the God of Israel, “Why have You not answered \zwd Your servant|Q28730\zwd* this day? If the fault is with me or \zwd my son Jonathan|Q74891\zwd*, respond with Urim, but if the fault is with the men of Israel, respond with Thummim.”\f + \fr 14:41 \ft LXX and Vulgate; MT contains only the short quotation, “Give a perfect (lot).”\f* And \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* and \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* were selected, but the people were cleared of the charge. 
\b
\m 
\v 42 Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* said, “Cast the lot between me and \zwd my son Jonathan|Q74891\zwd*.” And \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* was selected. 
\b
\m 
\v 43 “Tell me what you have done,” \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* commanded him. 
\b
\m So \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* told him, “I only tasted a little honey with the end of the staff that was in my hand. And now I must die?” 
\b
\m 
\v 44 And \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* declared, “May God punish me, and ever so severely, if you, \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd*, do not surely die!” 
\b
\m 
\v 45 But the people said to \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, “Must \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* die—he who accomplished such a great deliverance for Israel? Never! As surely as the LORD lives, not a hair of his head will fall to the ground, for with God’s help he has accomplished this today.” 
\b
\m So the people rescued \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd*, and he did not die. 
\v 46 Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* gave up his pursuit of the Philistines, and the Philistines returned to their own land. 
\s1 Saul’s Victories
\b
\m 
\v 47 After \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* had assumed the kingship over Israel, he fought against all his enemies on every side—the Moabites, the Ammonites, the Edomites, \zwd the kings \f + \fr 14:47 \ft MT; DSS and LXX king\f* of Zobah|\zwd*, and the Philistines. Wherever he turned, he routed them.\f + \fr 14:47 \ft Or he inflicted punishment on them from Hebrew; LXX he was victorious\f* 
\v 48 He fought valiantly and defeated the Amalekites, delivering Israel from the hands of its plunderers. 
\b
\m 
\v 49 Now the sons of \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* were \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd*, \zwd Ishvi|Q132131762\zwd*, and \zwd Malchishua|Q132132935\zwd*. His two daughters were named \zwd Merab|Q10581739\zwd* (his firstborn) and \zwd Michal|Q1341397\zwd* (his younger daughter). 
\v 50 His wife’s name was \zwd Ahinoam daughter of \zwd Ahimaaz|id="Q114833216" t="2"\zwd*|Q9138866\zwd*. The name of the commander of his army was \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd*, the son of \zwd \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*’s uncle Ner|id="Q132145387" t="2"\zwd*. 
\v 51 \zwd \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*’s father Kish|id="Q2915270" t="2"\zwd* and \zwd \zwd Abner|id="Q1133337" t="2"\zwd*’s father Ner|id="Q132145387" t="2"\zwd* were sons of \zwd Abiel|id="Q16128781" t="2"\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 52 And the war with the Philistines was fierce for all the days of \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*. So whenever he noticed any strong or brave man, \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* would enlist him. 
\c 15
\s1 Saul’s Disobedience
\m 
\v 1 Then \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* said to \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, “The LORD sent me to anoint you king over His people Israel. Now therefore, listen to the words of the LORD. 
\v 2 This is what the LORD of Hosts says: ‘I witnessed what the Amalekites did to the Israelites when they opposed them on their way up from Egypt. 
\v 3 Now go and attack the Amalekites and devote to destruction \f + \fr 15:3 \ft Forms of the Hebrew cherem refer to the giving over of things or persons to the LORD, either by destroying them or by giving them as an offering; also in verses 8, 9, 15, 18, 20, and 21.\f* all that belongs to them. Do not spare them, but put to death men and women, children and infants, oxen and sheep, camels and donkeys.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 So \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* summoned the troops and numbered them at Telaim—200,000 foot soldiers and 10,000 men of Judah. 
\v 5 \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* came to the city of Amalek and lay in wait in the valley. 
\v 6 And he warned the Kenites, “Since you showed kindness to all the Israelites when they came up out of Egypt, go on and get away from the Amalekites. Otherwise I will sweep you away with them.” 
\b
\m So the Kenites moved away from the Amalekites. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* struck down the Amalekites all the way from Havilah to Shur, which is east of Egypt. 
\v 8 He captured \zwd Agag king of Amalek|Q390086\zwd* alive, but devoted all the others to destruction with the sword. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* and his troops spared \zwd Agag|Q390086\zwd*, along with the best of the sheep and cattle, the fat calves \f + \fr 15:9 \ft Or the grown bulls\f* and lambs, and the best of everything else. They were unwilling to devote them to destruction, but they devoted to destruction all that was despised and worthless. 
\s1 Samuel Denounces Saul
\b
\m 
\v 10 Then the word of the LORD came to \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*, saying, 
\v 11 “I regret that I have made \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* king, for he has turned away from following Me and has not carried out My instructions.” 
\b
\m And \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* was distressed and cried out to the LORD all that night. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 Early in the morning \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* got up to confront \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, but he was told, “\zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* has gone to Carmel, and behold, he has set up a monument for himself and has turned and gone down to Gilgal.” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 When \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* reached him, \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* said to him, “May the LORD bless you. I have carried out the LORD’s instructions.” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 But \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* replied, “Then what is this bleating of sheep and lowing of cattle that I hear?” 
\b
\m 
\v 15 \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* answered, “The troops brought them from the Amalekites; they spared the best sheep and cattle to sacrifice to the LORD your God, but the rest we devoted to destruction.” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 “Stop!” exclaimed \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*. “Let me tell you what the LORD said to me last night.” 
\b
\m “Tell me,” \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 And \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* said, “Although you were once small in your own eyes, have you not become the head of the tribes of Israel? The LORD anointed you king over Israel 
\v 18 and sent you on a mission, saying, ‘Go and devote to destruction the sinful Amalekites. Fight against them until you have wiped them out.’ 
\v 19 So why did you not obey the LORD? Why did you rush upon the plunder and do evil in the sight of the LORD?” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 “But I did obey the LORD,” \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* replied. “I went on the mission that the LORD gave me. I brought back \zwd Agag king of Amalek|Q390086\zwd* and devoted the Amalekites to destruction. 
\v 21 The troops took sheep and cattle from the plunder, the best of the things devoted to destruction, in order to sacrifice them to the LORD your God at Gilgal.” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 But \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* declared: 
\b
\q1 “Does the LORD delight in burnt offerings and sacrifices 
\q2 as much as in obedience to His voice? 
\q1 Behold, obedience is better than sacrifice, 
\q2 and attentiveness is better than the fat of rams. 
\q1 
\v 23 For rebellion is like the sin of divination, 
\q2 and arrogance is like the wickedness of idolatry. 
\q1 Because you have rejected the word of the LORD, 
\q2 He has rejected you as king.” 
\s1 Saul’s Confession
\b
\m 
\v 24 Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* said to \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*, “I have sinned; I have transgressed the LORD’s commandment and your instructions, because I feared the people and obeyed their voice. 
\v 25 Now therefore, please forgive my sin and return with me so I can worship the LORD.” 
\b
\m 
\v 26 “I will not return with you,” \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* replied. “For you have rejected the word of the LORD, and He has rejected you as king over Israel.” 
\b
\m 
\v 27 As \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* turned to go, \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* grabbed the hem of his robe, and it tore. 
\v 28 So \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* said to him, “The LORD has torn the kingdom of Israel from you today and has given it to your neighbor who is better than you. 
\v 29 Moreover, the Glory of Israel does not lie or change His mind, for He is not a man, that He should change His mind.” 
\b
\m 
\v 30 “I have sinned,” \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* replied. “Please honor me now before the elders of my people and before Israel. Come back with me, so that I may worship the LORD your God.” 
\b
\m 
\v 31 So \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* went back with \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, and \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* worshiped the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 32 Then \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* said, “Bring me \zwd Agag king of the Amalekites|Q390086\zwd*.” 
\b
\m \zwd Agag|Q390086\zwd* came to him cheerfully,\f + \fr 15:32 \ft Or cautiously or in chains; see DSS and LXX.\f* for he thought, “Surely the bitterness of death is past.”\f + \fr 15:32 \ft Or “Surely this is the bitterness of death.” See DSS and LXX.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 33 But \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* declared: 
\b
\q1 “As your sword has made women childless, 
\q2 so your mother will be childless among women.” 
\b
\m And \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* hacked \zwd Agag|Q390086\zwd* to pieces before the LORD at Gilgal. 
\b
\m 
\v 34 Then \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* went to Ramah, but \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* went up to his home in Gibeah of \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*. 
\v 35 And to the day of his death, \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* never again visited \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*. \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* mourned for \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, and the LORD regretted that He had made \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* king over Israel. 
\c 16
\s1 Samuel Anoints David
\m 
\v 1 Now the LORD said to \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*, “How long are you going to mourn for \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, since I have rejected him as king over Israel? Fill your horn with oil and go. I am sending you to \zwd Jesse of Bethlehem|id="Q45090" t="2"\zwd*, for I have selected from his sons a king for Myself.” 
\b
\m 
\v 2 “How can I go?” \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* asked. “\zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* will hear of it and kill me!” 
\b
\m The LORD answered, “Take a heifer with you and say, ‘I have come to sacrifice to the LORD.’ 
\v 3 Then invite \zwd Jesse|id="Q45090" t="2"\zwd* to the sacrifice, and I will show you what you are to do. You are to anoint for Me the one I indicate.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 So \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* did what the LORD had said and went to Bethlehem. When the elders of the town met him, they trembled and asked, “Do you come in peace?” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 “In peace,” he replied. “I have come to sacrifice to the LORD. Consecrate yourselves and come with me to the sacrifice.” 
\b
\m Then he consecrated \zwd Jesse|Q45090\zwd* and \zwd his sons|\zwd* and invited them to the sacrifice. 
\v 6 When they arrived, \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* saw \zwd Eliab|\zwd* and said, “Surely here before the LORD is His anointed.” 
\b
\m 
\v 7 But the LORD said to \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*, “Do not consider his appearance or height, for I have rejected him; the LORD does not see as man does. For man sees the outward appearance, but the LORD sees the heart.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 Then \zwd Jesse|Q45090\zwd* called \zwd Abinadab|Q11903854\zwd* and presented him to \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*, who said, “The LORD has not chosen this one either.” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 Next \zwd Jesse|Q45090\zwd* presented \zwd Shammah|Q16130530\zwd*,\f + \fr 16:9 \ft Shammah is a variant of Shimeah, Shimea, and Shimei; see 2 Samuel 13:3, 2 Samuel 21:21, and 1 Chronicles 2:13.\f* but \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* said, “The LORD has not chosen this one either.” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Thus \zwd Jesse|Q45090\zwd* made \zwd seven of his sons|\zwd* pass before \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*, but \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* told him, “The LORD has not chosen any of these.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 And \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* asked him, “Are these all the sons you have?” 
\b
\m “There is still \zwd the youngest|Q41370\zwd*,” \zwd Jesse|Q45090\zwd* replied, “but he is tending the sheep.” 
\b
\m “Send for him,” \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* replied. “For we will not sit down to eat until he arrives.” 
\b
\m 
\v 12 So \zwd Jesse|Q45090\zwd* sent for \zwd his youngest son|Q41370\zwd* and brought him in. He was ruddy, with beautiful eyes and a handsome appearance. And the LORD said, “Rise and anoint him, for he is the one.” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 So \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* took the horn of oil and anointed him in the presence of his brothers, and the Spirit of the LORD rushed upon \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* from that day forward. Then \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* set out and went to Ramah. 
\s1 David Serves Saul
\b
\m 
\v 14 Now the Spirit of the LORD departed from \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, and a spirit of distress \f + \fr 16:14 \ft Or a harmful spirit; similarly in verses 15, 16, and 23\f* from the LORD began to torment him. 
\v 15 \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*’s servants said to him, “Surely a spirit of distress from God is tormenting you. 
\v 16 Let our lord command your servants here to seek out someone who can skillfully play the harp. Whenever the spirit of distress from God is upon you, he is to play it, and you will be well.” 
\b
\m 
\v 17 And \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* commanded his servants, “Find me someone who plays well, and bring him to me.” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 One of the servants answered, “I have seen \zwd a son of \zwd Jesse of Bethlehem|id="Q45090" t="2"\zwd*|Q41370\zwd* who knows how to play the harp. He is a mighty man of valor, a warrior, eloquent and handsome, and the LORD is with him.” 
\b
\m 
\v 19 So \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* sent messengers to \zwd Jesse|Q45090\zwd* and said, “Send me \zwd your son David|Q41370\zwd*, who is with the sheep.” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 And \zwd Jesse|Q45090\zwd* took a donkey loaded with bread, a skin of wine, and one young goat and sent them to \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* with \zwd his son David|Q41370\zwd*. 
\v 21 When \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* came to \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* and entered his service, \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* loved him very much, and \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* became his armor-bearer. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* sent word to \zwd Jesse|Q45090\zwd*, saying, “Let \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* remain in my service, for I am pleased with him.” 
\v 23 And whenever the spirit from God came upon \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* would pick up his harp and play. Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* would find relief and feel better, and the spirit of distress would depart from him. 
\c 17
\s1 Goliath’s Challenge
\m 
\v 1 Now the Philistines gathered their forces for war at Socoh in Judah, and they camped between Socoh and Azekah in Ephes-dammim. 
\v 2 \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* and the men of Israel assembled and camped in the Valley of Elah, arraying themselves for battle against the Philistines. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 The Philistines stood on one hill and the Israelites stood on another, with the valley between them. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Then a champion named \zwd Goliath|Q192785\zwd*, who was from Gath, came out from the Philistine camp. He was six cubits and a span in height,\f + \fr 17:4 \ft Goliath was approximately 9 feet 9 inches or 297 centimeters tall; LXX, DSS, and Josephus four cubits and a span in height (approximately 6 feet 9 inches or 206 centimeters tall).\f* 
\v 5 and he had a bronze helmet on his head. He wore a bronze coat of mail weighing five thousand shekels,\f + \fr 17:5 \ft 5,000 shekels is approximately 125.6 pounds or 57 kilograms.\f* 
\v 6 and he had armor of bronze on his legs and a javelin of bronze slung between his shoulders. 
\v 7 The shaft of his spear was like a weaver’s beam, and its iron point weighed six hundred shekels.\f + \fr 17:7 \ft 600 shekels is approximately 15.1 pounds or 6.8 kilograms.\f* In addition, his shield bearer went before him. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 And \zwd Goliath|Q192785\zwd* stood and shouted to the ranks of Israel, “Why do you come out and array yourselves for battle? Am I not a Philistine, and are you not servants of \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*? Choose one of your men and have him come down against me. 
\v 9 If he is able to fight me and kill me, then we will be your servants. But if I prevail against him and kill him, then you shall be our servants and work for us.” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Then the Philistine said, “I defy the ranks of Israel this day! Give me a man to fight!” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 On hearing the words of the Philistine, \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* and all the Israelites were dismayed and greatly afraid. 
\s1 David Accepts the Challenge
\b
\m 
\v 12 Now \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* was the son of a man named \zwd Jesse|Q45090\zwd*, an Ephrathite from Bethlehem of Judah who had eight sons. And in the days of \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, \zwd Jesse|Q45090\zwd* was old and well along in years.\f + \fr 17:12 \ft LXX and Syriac; Hebrew he had become advanced among men\f* 
\v 13 \zwd The three older sons of \zwd Jesse|Q45090\zwd*|Q96105737,Q11903854,Q16130530\zwd* had followed \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* into battle: The firstborn was \zwd Eliab|Q96105737\zwd*, the second was \zwd Abinadab|Q11903854\zwd*, and the third was \zwd Shammah|Q16130530\zwd*. 
\v 14 And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* was the youngest. 
\b
\m \zwd The three oldest|Q96105737,Q11903854,Q16130530\zwd* had followed \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, 
\v 15 but \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* went back and forth from \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* to tend \zwd his father|id="Q45090" t="2"\zwd*’s sheep in Bethlehem. 
\b
\m 
\v 16 For forty days the Philistine came forward every morning and evening to take his stand. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 One day \zwd Jesse|Q45090\zwd* said to \zwd his son David|Q41370\zwd*, “Take this ephah of roasted grain \f + \fr 17:17 \ft An ephah is approximately 20 dry quarts or 22 liters of roasted grain.\f* and these ten loaves of bread for \zwd your brothers|Q96105737,Q11903854,Q16130530\zwd* and hurry to their camp. 
\v 18 Take also these ten portions of cheese to the commander of their unit. Check on the welfare of \zwd your brothers|Q96105737,Q11903854,Q16130530\zwd* and bring back an assurance from them.\f + \fr 17:18 \ft Or some token from them or some pledge from them\f* 
\v 19 They are with \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* and all the men of Israel in the Valley of Elah, fighting against the Philistines.” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* got up early in the morning, left the flock with a keeper, loaded up, and set out as \zwd Jesse|Q45090\zwd* had instructed him. He reached the camp as the army was marching out to its position and shouting the battle cry. 
\v 21 And Israel and the Philistines arrayed in formation against each other. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* left his supplies in the care of the quartermaster and ran to the battle line. When he arrived, he asked \zwd his brothers|Q96105737,Q11903854,Q16130530\zwd* how they were doing. 
\v 23 And as he was speaking with them, suddenly the champion named \zwd Goliath|Q192785\zwd*, the Philistine from Gath, came forward from the ranks of the Philistines and shouted his usual words, which \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* also heard. 
\b
\m 
\v 24 When all the men of Israel saw \zwd Goliath|Q192785\zwd*, they fled from him in great fear. 
\b
\m 
\v 25 Now the men of Israel had been saying, “Do you see how \zwd this man|Q192785\zwd* keeps coming out to defy Israel? To the man who kills him \zwd the king|Q28730\zwd* will give great riches. And he will give him \zwd his daughter|\zwd* in marriage and exempt his father’s house from taxation in Israel.” 
\b
\m 
\v 26 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* asked the men who were standing with him, “What will be done for the man who kills this Philistine and removes this disgrace from Israel? Just who is this uncircumcised Philistine, that he should defy the armies of the living God?” 
\b
\m 
\v 27 The people told him about the offer, saying, “That is what will be done for the man who kills him.” 
\b
\m 
\v 28 Now when \zwd \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s oldest brother Eliab|Q96105737\zwd* heard him speaking to the men, his anger burned against \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*. “Why have you come down here?” he asked. “And with whom did you leave those few sheep in the wilderness? I know your pride and wickedness of heart—you have come down to see the battle!” 
\b
\m 
\v 29 “What have I done now?” said \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*. “Was it not just a question?” 
\v 30 Then he turned from him toward another and asked about the offer, and those people answered him just as the first ones had answered. 
\b
\m 
\v 31 Now \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s words were overheard and reported to \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, who sent for him. 
\b
\m 
\v 32 And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* said to \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, “Let no man’s heart fail on account of this Philistine. \zwd Your servant|Q41370\zwd* will go and fight him!” 
\b
\m 
\v 33 But \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* replied, “You cannot go out against this Philistine to fight him. You are just a boy, and he has been a warrior from his youth.” 
\b
\m 
\v 34 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* replied, “\zwd Your servant|Q41370\zwd* has been tending \zwd his father|id="Q45090" t="2"\zwd*’s sheep, and whenever a lion or a bear came and carried off a lamb from the flock, 
\v 35 I went after it, struck it down, and delivered the lamb from its mouth. If it reared up against me, I would grab it by its fur, strike it down, and kill it. 
\v 36 \zwd Your servant|Q41370\zwd* has killed lions and bears; this uncircumcised Philistine will be like one of them, for he has defied the armies of the living God.” 
\b
\m 
\v 37 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* added, “The LORD, who delivered me from the claws of the lion and the bear, will deliver me from the hand of this Philistine.” 
\b
\m “Go,” said \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, “and may the LORD be with you.” 
\s1 David Slays Goliath
\b
\m 
\v 38 Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* clothed \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* in his own tunic, put a bronze helmet on his head, and dressed him in armor. 
\v 39 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* strapped his sword over the tunic and tried to walk, but he was not accustomed to them. 
\b
\m “I cannot walk in these,” \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* said to \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*. “I am not accustomed to them.” So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* took them off. 
\v 40 And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* took his staff in his hand, selected five smooth stones from the brook, and put them in the pouch of his shepherd’s bag. And with his sling in hand, he approached the Philistine. 
\b
\m 
\v 41 Now \zwd the Philistine|Q192785\zwd* came closer and closer to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, with his shield-bearer before him. 
\v 42 When \zwd the Philistine|Q192785\zwd* looked and saw \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, he despised him because he was just a boy, ruddy and handsome. 
\v 43 “Am I a dog,” he said to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, “that you come at me with sticks?” And \zwd the Philistine|Q192785\zwd* cursed \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* by his gods. 
\v 44 “Come here,” he called to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, “and I will give your flesh to the birds of the air and the beasts of the field!” 
\b
\m 
\v 45 But \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* said to \zwd the Philistine|Q192785\zwd*, “You come against me with sword and spear and javelin, but I come against you in the name of the LORD of Hosts, the God of the armies of Israel, whom you have defied. 
\v 46 This day the LORD will deliver you into my hand. This day I will strike you down, cut off your head, and give the carcasses of the Philistine army to the birds of the air and the creatures of the earth. Then the whole world will know that there is a God in Israel. 
\v 47 And all those assembled here will know that it is not by sword or spear that the LORD saves; for the battle is the LORD’s, and He will give all of you into our hands.” 
\b
\m 
\v 48 As \zwd the Philistine|Q192785\zwd* started forward to attack him, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* ran quickly toward the battle line to meet him. 
\v 49 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* reached into his bag, took out a stone, and slung it, striking \zwd the Philistine|Q192785\zwd* on the forehead. The stone sank into his forehead, and he fell facedown on the ground. 
\b
\m 
\v 50 Thus \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* prevailed over \zwd the Philistine|Q192785\zwd* with a sling and a stone; without a sword in his hand he struck down \zwd the Philistine|Q192785\zwd* and killed him. 
\v 51 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* ran and stood over him. He grabbed \zwd the Philistine|Q192785\zwd*’s sword and pulled it from its sheath and killed him, and he cut off his head with the sword. 
\b
\m When the Philistines saw that \zwd their hero|id="Q192785" t="2"\zwd* was dead, they turned and ran. 
\v 52 Then the men of Israel and Judah charged forward with a shout and pursued the Philistines to the entrance of Gath \f + \fr 17:52 \ft LXX; Hebrew of Gai; that is, of the valley\f* and to the gates of Ekron. And the bodies of the Philistines were strewn along the Shaaraim road to Gath and Ekron. 
\b
\m 
\v 53 When the Israelites returned from their pursuit of the Philistines, they plundered their camps. 
\v 54 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* took the head of the Philistine and brought it to Jerusalem, and he put \zwd Goliath|id="Q192785" t="2"\zwd*’s weapons in his own tent. 
\b
\m 
\v 55 As \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* had watched \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* going out to confront \zwd the Philistine|id="Q192785" t="2"\zwd*, he said to \zwd Abner the commander of the army|\zwd*, “\zwd Abner|\zwd*, whose son is \zwd this young man|Q41370\zwd*?” 
\b
\m “As surely as you live, O king,” \zwd Abner|\zwd* replied, “I do not know.” 
\b
\m 
\v 56 “Find out whose son \zwd this young man|Q41370\zwd* is!” said \zwd the king|Q28730\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 57 So when \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* returned from killing \zwd the Philistine|id="Q192785" t="2"\zwd*, still holding his head in his hand, \zwd Abner|\zwd* took him and brought him before \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 58 “Whose son are you, \zwd young man|Q41370\zwd*?” asked \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*. 
\b
\m “I am the son of \zwd your servant Jesse|id="Q45090" t="2"\zwd* of Bethlehem,” \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* replied. 
\c 18
\s1 Jonathan Befriends David
\m 
\v 1 After \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* had finished speaking with \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, the souls of \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* and \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* were knit together, and \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* loved him as himself. 
\v 2 And from that day \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* kept \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* with him and did not let him return to \zwd his father|id="Q45090" t="2"\zwd*’s house. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 Then \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* made a covenant with \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* because he loved him as himself. 
\v 4 And \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* removed the robe he was wearing and gave it to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, along with his tunic, his sword, his bow, and his belt. 
\s1 Saul Envies David
\b
\m 
\v 5 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* marched out and prospered in everything \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* sent him to do, and \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* set him over the men of war. And this was pleasing in the sight of all the people, and of \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*’s officers as well. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 As the troops were returning home after \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* had killed \zwd the Philistine|id="Q192785" t="2"\zwd*, the women came out of all the cities of Israel to meet \zwd King Saul|Q28730\zwd* with singing and dancing, with joyful songs, and with tambourines and other instruments.\f + \fr 18:6 \ft Possibly three-stringed instruments or cymbals or lutes or lyres \f* 
\v 7 And as the women danced, they sang out: 
\b
\q1 “\zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* has slain his thousands, 
\q2 and \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* his tens of thousands.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 And \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* was furious and resented this song. “They have ascribed tens of thousands to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*,” he said, “but only thousands to me. What more can he have but the kingdom?” 
\v 9 And from that day forward \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* kept a jealous eye on \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 The next day a spirit of distress \f + \fr 18:10 \ft Or a harmful spirit\f* sent from God came upon \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, and he prophesied inside the house while \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* played the harp as usual. Now \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* was holding a spear, 
\v 11 and he hurled it, thinking, “I will pin \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* to the wall.” But \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* eluded him twice. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 So \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* was afraid of \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, because the LORD was with \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* but had departed from \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*. 
\v 13 Therefore \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* sent \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* away and gave him command of a thousand men. \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* led the troops out to battle and back, 
\v 14 and he continued to prosper in all his ways, because the LORD was with him. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 When \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* saw that \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* was very successful, he was afraid of him. 
\v 16 But all Israel and Judah loved \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, because he was leading them out to battle and back. 
\s1 David Marries Michal
\b
\m 
\v 17 Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* said to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, “Here is my older daughter \zwd Merab|Q10581739\zwd*. I will give her to you in marriage. Only be valiant for me and fight the LORD’s battles.” But \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* was thinking, “I need not raise my hand against him; let the hand of the Philistines be against him.” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* said to \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, “Who am I, and what is my family or \zwd my father|\zwd*’s clan in Israel, that I should become the son-in-law of \zwd the king|Q28730\zwd*?” 
\v 19 So when it was time \f + \fr 18:19 \ft Or But when it was time\f* to give \zwd \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*’s daughter Merab|Q10581739\zwd* to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, she was given in marriage to \zwd Adriel of Meholah|Q2825091\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 Now \zwd \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*’s daughter Michal|Q1341397\zwd* loved \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, and when this was reported to \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, it pleased him. 
\v 21 “I will give her to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*,” \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* thought, “so that she may be a snare to him, and the hand of the Philistines may be against him.” So \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* said to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, “For a second time now you can be my son-in-law.” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* ordered his servants, “Speak to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* privately and tell him, ‘Behold, \zwd the king|Q28730\zwd* is pleased with you, and all his servants love you. Now therefore, become his son-in-law.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 23 But when \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*’s servants relayed these words to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, he replied, “Does it seem trivial in your sight to be the son-in-law of \zwd the king|Q28730\zwd*? I am a poor man and lightly esteemed.” 
\b
\m 
\v 24 And the servants told \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* what \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* had said. 
\b
\m 
\v 25 \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* replied, “Say to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, ‘\zwd The king|Q28730\zwd* desires no other dowry but a hundred Philistine foreskins as revenge on his enemies.’” But \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* intended to cause \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s death at the hands of the Philistines. 
\b
\m 
\v 26 When the servants reported these terms to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, he was pleased to become \zwd the king|Q28730\zwd*’s son-in-law. Before the wedding day arrived, 
\v 27 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and his men went out and killed two hundred Philistines. He brought their foreskins and presented them as payment in full to become \zwd the king|Q28730\zwd*’s son-in-law. Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* gave \zwd his daughter Michal|Q1341397\zwd* to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* in marriage. 
\b
\m 
\v 28 When \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* realized that the LORD was with \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and that \zwd his daughter Michal|Q1341397\zwd* loved \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, 
\v 29 he grew even more afraid of \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*. So from then on \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* was \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s enemy. 
\b
\m 
\v 30 Every time the Philistine commanders came out for battle, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* was more successful than all of \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*’s officers, so that his name was highly esteemed. 
\c 19
\s1 Saul Tries to Kill David
\r (Psalms 59:1–17)
\m
\v 1 Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* ordered \zwd his son Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* and all his servants to kill \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*.
\b
\m But \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* delighted greatly in \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*,
\v 2 so he warned \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, saying, “\zwd My father Saul|Q28730\zwd* intends to kill you. Be on your guard in the morning; find a secret place and hide there.
\v 3 I will go out and stand beside \zwd my father|Q28730\zwd* in the field where you are, so I can ask about you. And if I find out anything, I will tell you.”
\b
\m
\v 4 Then \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* spoke well of \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* to \zwd his father Saul|Q28730\zwd* and said to him, “\zwd The king|Q28730\zwd* should not sin against \zwd his servant David|Q41370\zwd*; he has not sinned against you. In fact, his actions have been highly beneficial to you.
\v 5 He took his life in his hands when he struck down \zwd the Philistine|id="Q192785" t="2"\zwd*, and the LORD worked a great salvation for all Israel. You saw it and rejoiced, so why would you sin against innocent blood by killing \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* for no reason?”
\b
\m
\v 6 \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* listened to the voice of \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* and swore an oath: “As surely as the LORD lives, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* will not be put to death.”
\b
\m
\v 7 So \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* summoned \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and told him all these things. Then \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* brought \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* to \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, and \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* was with \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* as before.
\b
\m
\v 8 When war broke out again, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* went out and fought the Philistines and struck them with such a mighty blow that they fled before him.
\b
\m
\v 9 But as \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* was sitting in his house with his spear in his hand, a spirit of distress \f + \fr 19:9 \ft Or a harmful spirit\f* from the LORD came upon him. While \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* was playing the harp,
\v 10 \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* tried to pin him to the wall with his spear. But \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* eluded him and the spear struck the wall. And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* fled and escaped that night.
\b
\m
\v 11 Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* sent messengers to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s house to watch him and kill him in the morning. But \zwd \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s wife Michal|Q1341397\zwd* warned him, “If you do not run for your life tonight, tomorrow you will be dead!”
\v 12 So \zwd Michal|Q1341397\zwd* lowered \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* from the window, and he ran away and escaped.
\b
\m
\v 13 Then \zwd Michal|Q1341397\zwd* took a household idol \f + \fr 19:13 \ft Or a household god; Hebrew teraphim; also in verse 16\f* and laid it in the bed, placed some goat hair on its head, and covered it with a garment.
\v 14 When \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* sent the messengers to seize \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, \zwd Michal|Q1341397\zwd* said, “He is ill.”
\b
\m
\v 15 But \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* sent the messengers back to see \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and told them, “Bring him up to me in his bed so I can kill him.”
\v 16 And when the messengers entered, there was the idol in the bed with the goat hair on its head.
\b
\m
\v 17 And \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* said to \zwd Michal|Q1341397\zwd*, “Why did you deceive me like this? You sent my enemy away, and he has escaped!”
\b
\m \zwd Michal|Q1341397\zwd* replied, “He said to me, ‘Help me get away, or I will kill you!’”
\b
\m
\v 18 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* ran away and escaped. And he went to \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* at Ramah and told him all that \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* had done to him. Then he and \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* went to Naioth and stayed there.
\b
\m
\v 19 When \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* was told that \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* was at Naioth in Ramah,
\v 20 he sent messengers to seize him. But when they saw the group of prophets prophesying, with \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* leading them, the Spirit of God came upon them, and \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*’s messengers also began to prophesy.
\b
\m
\v 21 When this was reported to \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, he sent more messengers, but they began to prophesy as well.
\b
\m So \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* tried again and sent messengers a third time, and even they began to prophesy.
\b
\m
\v 22 Finally, \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* himself left for Ramah and came to the large cistern at Secu, where he asked, “Where are \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* and \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*?”
\b
\m “At Naioth in Ramah,” he was told.
\b
\m
\v 23 So \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* went to Naioth in Ramah. But the Spirit of God came upon even \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, and he walked along prophesying until he came to Naioth in Ramah.
\v 24 Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* stripped off his robes and also prophesied before \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*. And he collapsed and lay naked all that day and night. That is why it is said, “Is \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* also among the prophets?”
\c 20
\s1 Jonathan Helps David
\m 
\v 1 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* fled from Naioth in Ramah. He came to \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* and asked, “What have I done? What is my iniquity? How have I sinned against \zwd your father|Q28730\zwd*, that he wants to take my life?” 
\b
\m 
\v 2 “Far from it!” \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* replied. “You will not die. Indeed, my father does nothing, great or small, without telling me. So why would he hide this matter from me? This cannot be true!” 
\b
\m 
\v 3 But \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* again vowed, “\zwd Your father|Q28730\zwd* knows very well that I have found favor in your eyes, and he has said, ‘\zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* must not know of this, or he will be grieved.’ As surely as the LORD lives and as you yourself live, there is but a step between me and death.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Then \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* said to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, “Whatever you desire, I will do for you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* told him, “Look, tomorrow is the New Moon,\f + \fr 20:5 \ft That is, the New Moon feast; also in verses 18, 24, and 27\f* and I am supposed to dine with \zwd the king|Q28730\zwd*. Instead, let me go and hide in the field until the third evening from now. 
\v 6 If \zwd your father|Q28730\zwd* misses me at all, tell him, ‘\zwd David|Q41370\zwd* urgently requested my permission to hurry to Bethlehem, his hometown, because there is an annual sacrifice for his whole clan.’ 
\v 7 If he says, ‘Good,’ then \zwd your servant|Q41370\zwd* is safe, but if he is enraged, you will know he has evil intentions. 
\v 8 Therefore show kindness to \zwd your servant|Q41370\zwd*, for you have brought me into a covenant with you before the LORD. If there is iniquity in me, then kill me yourself; why should you bring me to \zwd your father|Q28730\zwd*?” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 “Never!” \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* replied. “If I ever found out that \zwd my father|Q28730\zwd* had evil intentions against you, would I not tell you?” 
\s1 Jonathan and David Renew Their Covenant
\b
\m 
\v 10 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* asked \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd*, “Who will tell me if \zwd your father|Q28730\zwd* answers you harshly?” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 “Come,” he replied, “let us go out to the field.” 
\b
\m So the two of them went out into the field, 
\v 12 and \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* said, “By the LORD, the God of Israel, I will sound out \zwd my father|Q28730\zwd* by this time tomorrow or the next day. If he is favorable toward you, will I not send for you and tell you? 
\v 13 But if \zwd my father|Q28730\zwd* intends to bring evil on you, then may the LORD punish me, and ever so severely, if I do not tell you and send you on your way in safety. May the LORD be with you, just as He has been with \zwd my father|Q28730\zwd*. 
\v 14 And as long as I live, treat me with the LORD’s loving devotion,\f + \fr 20:14 \ft Forms of the Hebrew chesed are translated here and in most cases throughout the Scriptures as loving devotion; the range of meaning includes love, goodness, kindness, faithfulness, and mercy, as well as loyalty to a covenant.\f* that I may not die, 
\v 15 and do not ever cut off your loving devotion from my household—not even when the LORD cuts off every one of \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s enemies from the face of the earth.” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 So \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* made a covenant with the house of \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, saying, “May the LORD hold \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s enemies accountable.” 
\v 17 And \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* had \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* reaffirm his vow out of love for him, for \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* loved \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* as he loved himself. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 Then \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* said to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, “Tomorrow is the New Moon, and you will be missed, because your seat will be empty. 
\v 19 When you have stayed three days, hurry down to the place you hid on the day this trouble began, and remain beside the stone Ezel.\f + \fr 20:19 \ft Ezel means departure.\f* 
\v 20 I will shoot three arrows to the side of it, as if I were aiming at a target. 
\v 21 Then I will send a boy and say, ‘Go, find the arrows!’ Now, if I expressly say to him, ‘Look, the arrows are on this side of you; bring them,’ then come, because as surely as the LORD lives, it is safe for you, and there is no danger. 
\v 22 But if I say to the young man, ‘Look, the arrows are beyond you,’ then you must go, for the LORD has sent you away. 
\v 23 And as for the matter you and I have discussed, the LORD is a witness between you and me forever.” 
\b
\m 
\v 24 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* hid in the field, and when the New Moon had come, \zwd the king|Q28730\zwd* sat down to eat. 
\v 25 He sat in his usual place by the wall, opposite \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* and beside \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd*,\f + \fr 20:25 \ft LXX; Hebrew by the wall. Jonathan arose and Abner sat down by Saul’s side,\f* but \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s place was empty. 
\v 26 \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* said nothing that day because he thought, “Something has happened to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* to make him ceremonially unclean—surely he is unclean.” 
\b
\m 
\v 27 But on the day after the New Moon, the second day, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s place was still empty, and \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* asked \zwd his son Jonathan|Q74891\zwd*, “Why hasn’t \zwd the son of \zwd Jesse|id="Q45090" t="2"\zwd*|Q41370\zwd* come to the meal either yesterday or today?” 
\b
\m 
\v 28 \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* answered, “\zwd David|Q41370\zwd* urgently requested my permission to go to Bethlehem, 
\v 29 saying, ‘Please let me go, because our clan is holding a sacrifice in the city, and \zwd my brother|\zwd* has told me to be there. So now, if I have found favor in your eyes, please let me go and see my brothers.’ That is why he has not come to \zwd the king|Q28730\zwd*’s table.” 
\s1 Saul Seeks to Kill Jonathan
\b
\m 
\v 30 Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*’s anger burned against \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd*, and he said to him, “You son of \zwd a perverse and rebellious woman|id="Q9138866" t="2"\zwd*! Do I not know that you have chosen \zwd the son of \zwd Jesse|id="Q45090" t="2"\zwd*|Q41370\zwd* to your own shame and to the shame of \zwd the mother who bore you|id="Q9138866" t="2"\zwd*? 
\v 31 For as long as \zwd the son of \zwd Jesse|id="Q45090" t="2"\zwd*|Q41370\zwd* lives on this earth, neither you nor your kingdom shall be established. Now send for him and bring him to me, for he must surely die!” 
\b
\m 
\v 32 “Why must he be put to death?” \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* replied. “What has he done?” 
\b
\m 
\v 33 Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* hurled his spear at \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* to kill him. So \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* knew that \zwd his father|Q28730\zwd* was determined to kill \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*. 
\v 34 \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* got up from the table in fierce anger and did not eat any food that second day of the month, for he was grieved by \zwd his father|Q28730\zwd*’s shameful treatment of \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 35 In the morning \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* went out to the field for the appointment with \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, and a small boy was with him. 
\v 36 He said to the boy, “Run and find the arrows I shoot.” And as the boy ran, \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* shot an arrow beyond him. 
\b
\m 
\v 37 When the boy reached the place where \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd*’s arrow had fallen, \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* called to him, “Isn’t the arrow beyond you?” 
\v 38 Then \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* cried out, “Hurry! Make haste! Do not delay!” So the boy picked up the arrow and returned to his master. 
\b
\m 
\v 39 But the boy did not know anything; only \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* and \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* knew the arrangement. 
\v 40 Then \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* gave his equipment to the boy and said, “Go, take it back to the city.” 
\b
\m 
\v 41 When the young man had gone, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* got up from the south side of the stone,\f + \fr 20:41 \ft Hebrew from the south side; LXX from beside the stone\f* fell facedown, and bowed three times. Then he and \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* kissed each other and wept together—though \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* wept more. 
\b
\m 
\v 42 And \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* said to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, “Go in peace, for the two of us have sworn in the name of the LORD, saying, ‘May the LORD be a witness between you and me, and between your descendants and mine forever.’” Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* got up and departed, and \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* went back into the city. 
\c 21
\s1 David Takes the Consecrated Bread
\r (Matthew 12:1–8; Mark 2:23–28; Luke 6:1–5)
\m 
\v 1 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* came to Nob, to \zwd Ahimelech the priest|Q400257\zwd*. And when \zwd Ahimelech|Q400257\zwd* met \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, he trembled and asked him, “Why are you alone? Why is no one with you?” 
\b
\m 
\v 2 “\zwd The king|Q28730\zwd* has given me a mission,” \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* replied. “He told me no one is to know about the mission on which I am sending you. And I have directed my young men to meet me at a certain place. 
\v 3 Now then, what do you have on hand? Give me five loaves of bread, or whatever can be found.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 “There is no common bread on hand,” \zwd the priest|Q400257\zwd* replied, “but there is some consecrated bread—provided that the young men have kept themselves from women.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* answered, “Women have indeed been kept from us, as is usual when I set out. And the bodies of the young men are holy even on common missions. How much more so today!” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 So \zwd the priest|Q400257\zwd* gave him the consecrated bread, since there was no bread there but the Bread of the Presence, which had been removed from before the LORD and replaced with hot bread on the day it was taken away. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 Now one of \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*’s servants was there that day, detained before the LORD. And his name was \zwd Doeg the Edomite|Q1234538\zwd*, the chief shepherd for \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*. 
\s1 David Flees to Gath
\r (Psalms 34:1–22; Psalms 56:1–13)
\b
\m 
\v 8 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* asked \zwd Ahimelech|Q400257\zwd*, “Is there not a spear or sword on hand here? For I have brought neither my sword nor my weapons with me, because \zwd the king|Q28730\zwd*’s mission was urgent.” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 \zwd The priest|Q400257\zwd* replied, “The sword of \zwd Goliath the Philistine|id="Q192785" t="2"\zwd*, whom you killed in the Valley of Elah, is here; it is wrapped in a cloth behind the ephod. If you want, you may take it. For there is no other but this one here.” 
\b
\m And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* said, “There is none like it; give it to me.” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 That day \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* fled from \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* and went to \zwd Achish king of Gath|\zwd*. 
\v 11 But the servants of \zwd Achish|\zwd* said to him, “Is this not \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, the king of the land? Did they not sing about him in their dances, saying: 
\b
\q1 ‘\zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* has slain his thousands, 
\q2 and \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* his tens of thousands’?” 
\b
\m 
\v 12 Now \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* took these words to heart and was very much afraid of \zwd Achish the king of Gath|\zwd*. 
\v 13 So he changed his behavior before them and feigned madness in their hands; he scratched on the doors of the gate and let his saliva run down his beard. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 Then \zwd Achish|\zwd* said to his servants, “Look, you can see that the man is insane! Why have you brought him to me? 
\v 15 Am I in need of madmen, that you have brought this man to rave in my presence? Must this man come into my house?” 
\c 22
\s1 David Flees to Adullam and Mizpeh
\r (Psalms 57:1–11; Psalms 142:1–7)
\m 
\v 1 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* left Gath and took refuge in the cave of Adullam. When \zwd his brothers|\zwd* and the rest of \zwd his father|Q45090\zwd*’s household heard about it, they went down to him there. 
\v 2 And all who were distressed or indebted or discontented rallied around him, and he became their leader. About four hundred men were with him. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 From there \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* went to Mizpeh of Moab, where he said to \zwd the king of Moab|\zwd*, “Please let \zwd my father and mother|Q45090,Q7041639\zwd* stay \f + \fr 22:3 \ft Syriac and Vulgate; Hebrew go forth\f* with you until I learn what God will do for me.” 
\v 4 So he left them in the care of \zwd the king of Moab|\zwd*, and they stayed with him the whole time \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* was in the stronghold. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 Then \zwd the prophet Gad|Q2559051\zwd* said to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, “Do not stay in the stronghold. Depart and go into the land of Judah.” So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* left and went to the forest of Hereth. 
\s1 Saul Slays the Priests of Nob
\r (Psalms 52:1–9)
\b
\m 
\v 6 Soon \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* learned that \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and his men had been discovered. At that time \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* was sitting under the tamarisk tree on the hill at Gibeah, with his spear in hand and all his servants standing around him. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* said to his servants, “Listen, men of Benjamin! Is \zwd the son of \zwd Jesse|id="Q45090" t="2"\zwd*|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* giving all of you fields and vineyards and making you commanders of thousands or hundreds? 
\v 8 Is that why all of you have conspired against me? Not one of you told me that \zwd my own son|Q74891\zwd* had made a covenant with \zwd the son of \zwd Jesse|id="Q45090" t="2"\zwd*|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*. Not one of you has shown concern for me or revealed to me that my son has stirred up my own servant to lie in wait against me, as is the case today.” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 But \zwd Doeg the Edomite|Q1234538\zwd*, who had stationed himself with \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*’s servants, answered: “I saw \zwd the son of \zwd Jesse|id="Q45090" t="2"\zwd*|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* come to \zwd Ahimelech son of \zwd Ahitub|\zwd*|Q400257\zwd* at Nob. 
\v 10 \zwd Ahimelech|Q400257\zwd* inquired of the LORD for him and gave him provisions. He also gave him the sword of \zwd Goliath the Philistine|id="Q192785" t="2"\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 Then \zwd the king|Q28730\zwd* sent messengers to summon \zwd Ahimelech the priest|Q400257\zwd*, the son of \zwd Ahitub|\zwd*, and \zwd his father|\zwd*’s whole family, who were priests at Nob. And all of them came to \zwd the king|Q28730\zwd*. 
\v 12 “Listen now, son of \zwd Ahitub|\zwd*,” said \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*. 
\b
\m “Here I am, my lord,” he replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 And \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* asked him, “Why have you and \zwd the son of \zwd Jesse|id="Q45090" t="2"\zwd*|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* conspired against me? You gave him bread and a sword and inquired of God for him so that he could rise up against me to lie in wait, as he is doing today.” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 \zwd Ahimelech|Q400257\zwd* answered \zwd the king|Q28730\zwd*, “Who among all your servants is as faithful as \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, \zwd \zwd the king|Q28730\zwd*’s son-in-law|Q41370\zwd*, \zwd the captain of your bodyguard|Q41370\zwd* and honored in your house? 
\v 15 Was that day the first time I inquired of God for him? Far be it from me! Let not \zwd the king|Q28730\zwd* accuse \zwd your servant|Q400257\zwd* or any of \zwd my father|\zwd*’s household, for \zwd your servant|Q400257\zwd* knew nothing of this whole affair—not in part or in whole.” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 But \zwd the king|Q28730\zwd* replied, “You will surely die, \zwd Ahimelech|Q400257\zwd*, you and all \zwd your father|\zwd*’s house!” 
\b
\m 
\v 17 Then \zwd the king|Q28730\zwd* ordered the guards at his side, “Turn and kill the priests of the LORD, because they too sided with \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*. For they knew he was fleeing, but they did not tell me.” 
\b
\m But \zwd the king|Q28730\zwd*’s servants would not lift a hand to strike the priests of the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 So \zwd the king|Q28730\zwd* ordered \zwd Doeg|Q1234538\zwd*, “You turn and strike down the priests!” 
\b
\m And \zwd Doeg the Edomite|Q1234538\zwd* turned and struck down the priests himself. On that day he killed eighty-five men who wore the linen ephod. 
\v 19 He also put to the sword Nob, the city of the priests, with its men and women, children and infants, oxen, donkeys, and sheep. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 But \zwd one of the sons of \zwd Ahimelech son of \zwd Ahitub|\zwd*|id="Q400257" t="2"\zwd*|id="Q321804" t="2"\zwd* escaped. His name was \zwd Abiathar|Q321804\zwd*, and he fled to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*. 
\v 21 And \zwd Abiathar|Q321804\zwd* told \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* that \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* had killed the priests of the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* said to \zwd Abiathar|Q321804\zwd*, “I knew that \zwd Doeg the Edomite|Q1234538\zwd* was there that day, and that he was sure to tell \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*. I myself am responsible for the lives of everyone in \zwd your father|Q321804\zwd*’s house. 
\v 23 Stay with me; do not be afraid, for he who seeks your life is seeking mine as well. You will be safe with me.” 
\c 23
\s1 David Delivers Keilah
\m 
\v 1 Now it was reported to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, “Look, the Philistines are fighting against Keilah and looting the threshing floors.” 
\b
\m 
\v 2 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* inquired of the LORD, “Should I go and attack these Philistines?” 
\b
\m And the LORD said to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, “Go and attack the Philistines and save Keilah.” 
\b
\m 
\v 3 But \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s men said to him, “Look, we are afraid here in Judah; how much more if we go to Keilah against the armies of the Philistines?” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Once again, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* inquired of the LORD, and the LORD answered him: “Go at once to Keilah, for I will deliver the Philistines into your hand.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and his men went to Keilah, fought against the Philistines, and carried off their livestock, striking them with a mighty blow. So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* saved the people of Keilah. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 (Now \zwd Abiathar son of \zwd Ahimelech|id="Q400257" t="2"\zwd*|Q321804\zwd* had brought the ephod with him when he fled to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* at Keilah.) 
\s1 Saul Pursues David
\r (Psalms 54:1–7)
\b
\m 
\v 7 When \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* was told that \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* had gone to Keilah, he said, “God has delivered him into my hand, for he has trapped himself by entering a town with gates and bars.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* summoned all his troops to go to war at Keilah and besiege \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and his men. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 When \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* learned that \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* was plotting evil against him, he said to \zwd Abiathar the priest|Q321804\zwd*, “Bring the ephod.” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* said, “O LORD, God of Israel, \zwd Your servant|Q41370\zwd* has heard that \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* intends to come to Keilah and destroy the city on my account. 
\v 11 Will the citizens of Keilah surrender me into his hand?\f + \fr 23:11 \ft Some manuscripts omit this question.\f* Will \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* come down, as \zwd Your servant|Q41370\zwd* has heard? O LORD, God of Israel, please tell \zwd Your servant|Q41370\zwd*.” 
\b
\m “He will,” said the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* asked, “Will the citizens of Keilah surrender me and my men into the hand of \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*?” 
\b
\m “They will,” said the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and his men, about six hundred strong, set out and departed from Keilah, moving from place to place. When \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* was told that \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* had escaped from Keilah, he declined to go forth. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* stayed in the wilderness strongholds and in the hill country of the Wilderness of Ziph. Day after day \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* searched for him, but God would not deliver \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* into his hand. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 While \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* was in Horesh in the Wilderness of Ziph, he saw that \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* had come out to take his life. 
\v 16 And \zwd \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*’s son Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* came to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* in Horesh and strengthened his hand in God, 
\v 17 saying, “Do not be afraid, for \zwd my father Saul|Q28730\zwd* will never lay a hand on you. And you will be king over Israel, and I will be your second-in-command. Even \zwd my father Saul|Q28730\zwd* knows this is true.” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 So the two of them made a covenant before the LORD. And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* remained in Horesh, while \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd* went home. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 Then the Ziphites came up to \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* at Gibeah and said, “Is not \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* hiding among us in the strongholds at Horesh, on the hill of Hachilah south of Jeshimon? 
\v 20 Now, O king, come down whenever your soul desires, and we will be responsible for delivering him into your hand.” 
\b
\m 
\v 21 “May you be blessed by the LORD,” replied \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, “for you have had compassion on me. 
\v 22 Please go and prepare further. Investigate and watch carefully where he goes and who has seen him there, for I am told that he is extremely cunning. 
\v 23 Observe and find out all the places where he hides. Then come back to me with certainty, and I will go with you. If he is in the land, I will search him out among all the clans of Judah.” 
\b
\m 
\v 24 So they set out and went to Ziph ahead of \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*. Now \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and his men were in the Wilderness of Maon in the Arabah south of Jeshimon, 
\v 25 and \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* and his men went to seek him. When \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* was told about it, he went down to the rock and stayed in the Wilderness of Maon. And when \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* heard of this, he pursued \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* there. 
\b
\m 
\v 26 \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* was proceeding along one side of the mountain, and \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and his men along the other side. Even though \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* was hurrying to get away, \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* and his men were closing in on \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and his men to capture them. 
\b
\m 
\v 27 Then a messenger came to \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, saying, “Come quickly, for the Philistines have raided the land!” 
\v 28 So \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* broke off his pursuit of \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and went to meet the Philistines. That is why that place is called Sela-hammahlekoth.\f + \fr 23:28 \ft Sela-hammahlekoth means Rock of Escape.\f* 
\v 29 And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* went up from there and lived in the strongholds of En-gedi. 
\c 24
\s1 David Spares Saul
\m 
\v 1 After \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* had returned from pursuing the Philistines, he was told, “\zwd David|Q41370\zwd* is in the wilderness of En-gedi.” 
\v 2 So \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* took three thousand chosen men from all Israel and went to look for \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and his men in the region of the Rocks of the Wild Goats. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 Soon \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* came to the sheepfolds along the road, where there was a cave, and he went in to relieve himself.\f + \fr 24:3 \ft Literally cover his feet, a euphemism for relieving oneself\f* And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and his men were hiding in the recesses of the cave. 
\v 4 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s men said to him, “This is the day about which the LORD said to you, ‘Behold, I will deliver your enemy into your hand, that you may do with him as you wish.’” 
\b
\m Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* crept up and stealthily cut off a corner of \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*’s robe. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 Afterward, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s conscience was stricken because he had cut off the corner of \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*’s robe. 
\v 6 So he said to his men, “The LORD forbid that I should do such a thing to \zwd my master|Q28730\zwd*, \zwd the LORD’s anointed|Q28730\zwd*. May I never lift my hand against him, since he is \zwd the LORD’s anointed|Q28730\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 7 With these words \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* restrained his men, and he did not let them rise up against \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*. Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* left the cave and went on his way. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 After that, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* got up, went out of the cave, and called out to \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, “\zwd My lord the king|Q28730\zwd*!” 
\b
\m When \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* looked behind him, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* bowed facedown in reverence 
\v 9 and said to \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, “Why do you listen to the words of men who say, ‘Look, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* intends to harm you’? 
\v 10 Behold, this day you have seen with your own eyes that the LORD delivered you into my hand in the cave. I was told to kill you, but I spared you and said, ‘I will not lift my hand against \zwd my lord|Q28730\zwd*, since he is \zwd the LORD’s anointed|Q28730\zwd*.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 11 See, \zwd my father|Q28730\zwd*, look at the corner of your robe in my hand. For I cut it off, but I did not kill you. Know and see that there is no evil or rebellion in my hands. I have not sinned against you, even though you are hunting me down to take my life. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 May the LORD judge between you and me, and may the LORD take vengeance on you, but my hand will never be against you. 
\v 13 As the old proverb says, ‘Wickedness proceeds from the wicked.’ But my hand will never be against you. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 Against whom has \zwd the king of Israel|Q28730\zwd* come out? Whom are you pursuing? A dead dog? A flea? 
\v 15 May the LORD be our judge and decide between you and me. May He take notice and plead my case and deliver me from your hand.” 
\s1 David’s Oath to Saul
\b
\m 
\v 16 When \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* had finished saying these things, \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* called back, “Is that your voice, \zwd David my son|Q41370\zwd*?” 
\b
\m Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* wept aloud 
\v 17 and said to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, “You are more righteous than I, for you have rewarded me with good, though I have rewarded you with evil. 
\v 18 And you have declared this day how you have treated me well, for when the LORD delivered me into your hand, you did not kill me. 
\v 19 When a man finds his enemy, does he let him go away unharmed? May the LORD reward you with good for what you have done for me this day. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 Now I know for sure that you will be king and that the kingdom of Israel will be established in your hands. 
\v 21 So now, swear to me by the LORD that you will not cut off my descendants or wipe out my name from \zwd my father|\zwd*’s house.” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* gave his oath to \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*. Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* returned home, but \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and his men went up to the stronghold. 
\c 25
\s1 The Death of Samuel
\m 
\v 1 \zdate|Q6577515:P570\*When \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* died, all Israel gathered to mourn for him; and they buried him at his home in Ramah. 
\b
\m Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* set out and went down to the Wilderness of Paran.\f + \fr 25:1 \ft Hebrew and some LXX manuscripts; other LXX manuscripts Maon\f* 
\s1 David, Nabal, and Abigail
\b
\m 
\v 2 Now there was \zwd a man|Q1709507\zwd* in Maon whose business was in Carmel. He was a very wealthy man with a thousand goats and three thousand sheep, which he was shearing in Carmel. 
\v 3 His name was \zwd Nabal|Q1709507\zwd*, and \zwd his wife|Q4416778\zwd*’s name was \zwd Abigail|Q4416778\zwd*. She was an intelligent and beautiful woman, but \zwd her husband|Q1709507\zwd*, a Calebite, was harsh and evil in his dealings. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 While \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* was in the wilderness, he heard that \zwd Nabal|Q1709507\zwd* was shearing sheep. 
\v 5 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* sent ten young men and instructed them, “Go up to \zwd Nabal|Q1709507\zwd* at Carmel. Greet him in my name 
\v 6 and say to him, ‘Long life to you, and peace to you and to your house and to all that belongs to you. 
\v 7 Now I hear that it is time for shearing. When your shepherds were with us, we did not harass them, and nothing of theirs was missing the whole time they were in Carmel. 
\v 8 Ask your young men, and they will tell you. So let my young men find favor with you, for we have come on the day of a feast. Please give whatever you can spare to your servants and to \zwd your son David|Q41370\zwd*.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 When \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s young men arrived, they relayed all these words to \zwd Nabal|Q1709507\zwd* on behalf of \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*. Then they waited. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 But \zwd Nabal|Q1709507\zwd* asked them, “Who is \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*? Who is \zwd this son of \zwd Jesse|id="Q45090" t="2"\zwd*|Q41370\zwd*? Many servants these days are breaking away from their masters. 
\v 11 Why should I take my bread and water and the meat I have slaughtered for my shearers, and give them to these men whose origin I do not know?” 
\b
\m 
\v 12 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s men turned around and went back, and they relayed to him all these words. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* said to his men, “Strap on your swords!” So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and all his men strapped on their swords, and about four hundred men followed \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, while two hundred stayed with the supplies. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 Meanwhile, one of \zwd Nabal|Q1709507\zwd*’s young men informed \zwd \zwd Nabal|Q1709507\zwd*’s wife Abigail|Q4416778\zwd*, “Look, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* sent messengers from the wilderness to greet our master, but he screamed at them. 
\v 15 Yet these men were very good to us. When we were in the field, we were not harassed, and nothing of ours went missing the whole time we lived among them. 
\v 16 They were a wall around us, both day and night, the whole time we were herding our sheep near them. 
\v 17 Now consider carefully what you must do, because disaster looms over \zwd our master|Q1709507\zwd* and all his household. For he is such a scoundrel that nobody can speak to him!” 
\s1 Abigail Intercedes for Nabal
\b
\m 
\v 18 Then \zwd Abigail|Q4416778\zwd* hurried and took two hundred loaves of bread, two skins of wine, five butchered sheep, five seahs of roasted grain,\f + \fr 25:18 \ft 5 seahs is approximately 33 dry quarts or 36.5 liters of roasted grain.\f* a hundred clusters of raisins, and two hundred cakes of figs. She loaded them on donkeys 
\v 19 and said to her young men, “Go ahead of me. I will be right behind you.” But she did not tell \zwd her husband Nabal|Q1709507\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 As \zwd Abigail|Q4416778\zwd* came riding her donkey into a mountain ravine, she saw \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and his men coming down toward her, and she met them. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 Now \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* had just said, “In vain I have protected all that belonged to \zwd this man|Q1709507\zwd* in the wilderness. Nothing that belongs to him has gone missing, yet he has paid me back evil for good. 
\v 22 May God punish \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*,\f + \fr 25:22 \ft Some LXX manuscripts; MT David’s enemies\f* and ever so severely, if I let one male belonging to \zwd Nabal|Q1709507\zwd* survive until morning.” 
\b
\m 
\v 23 When \zwd Abigail|Q4416778\zwd* saw \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, she quickly got off the donkey, fell facedown, and bowed before him. 
\v 24 She fell at his feet and said, “\zwd My lord|Q41370\zwd*, may the blame be on me alone, but please let \zwd your servant|Q4416778\zwd* speak to you; hear the words of \zwd your servant|Q4416778\zwd*. 
\v 25 \zwd My lord|Q41370\zwd* should pay no attention to \zwd this scoundrel Nabal|Q1709507\zwd*,\f + \fr 25:25 \ft Nabal means fool.\f* for he lives up to his name: His name means Fool, and folly accompanies him. I, \zwd your servant|Q4416778\zwd*, did not see \zwd my lord|Q41370\zwd*’s young men whom you sent. 
\b
\m 
\v 26 Now, \zwd my lord|Q41370\zwd*, as surely as the LORD lives and you yourself live, since the LORD has held you back from bloodshed and from avenging yourself with your own hand, may your enemies and those who seek harm for \zwd my lord|Q41370\zwd* be like \zwd Nabal|Q1709507\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 27 Now let this gift \zwd your servant|Q4416778\zwd* has brought to \zwd my lord|Q41370\zwd* be given to the young men who follow you. 
\v 28 Please forgive \zwd your servant|\zwd*’s offense, for the LORD will surely make a lasting dynasty for \zwd my lord|Q41370\zwd*, because he fights the LORD’s battles. May no evil be found in you as long as you live. 
\b
\m 
\v 29 And should someone pursue you and seek your life, then the life of \zwd my lord|Q41370\zwd* will be bound securely by the LORD your God in the bundle of the living. But He shall fling away the lives of your enemies like stones from a sling.\f + \fr 25:29 \ft Literally fling away the souls of your enemies as from the pocket of a sling\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 30 When the LORD has done for \zwd my lord|Q41370\zwd* all the good He promised, and when He has appointed you ruler over Israel, 
\v 31 then \zwd my lord|Q41370\zwd* will have no remorse or guilt of conscience over needless bloodshed and revenge. And when the LORD has dealt well with \zwd my lord|Q41370\zwd*, may you remember \zwd your servant|Q4416778\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 32 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* said to \zwd Abigail|Q4416778\zwd*, “Blessed be the LORD, the God of Israel, who sent you to meet me this day! 
\v 33 Blessed is your discernment, and blessed are you, because today you kept me from bloodshed and from avenging myself by my own hand. 
\v 34 Otherwise, as surely as the LORD, the God of Israel, lives, who has restrained me from harming you, if you had not come quickly to meet me, then surely no male belonging to \zwd Nabal|Q1709507\zwd* would have been left alive by morning light.” 
\b
\m 
\v 35 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* accepted from her hand what she had brought him, and he said to her, “Go home in peace. See, I have heeded your voice and granted your request.” 
\b
\m 
\v 36 When \zwd Abigail|Q4416778\zwd* returned to \zwd Nabal|Q1709507\zwd*, there he was in the house, holding a feast fit for a king, in high spirits and very drunk. So she told him nothing until morning light. 
\b
\m 
\v 37 In the morning when \zwd Nabal|Q1709507\zwd* was sober, \zwd his wife|Q4416778\zwd* told him about these events, and his heart failed within him, and he became like a stone. 
\v 38 About ten days later, the LORD struck \zwd Nabal|Q1709507\zwd* dead. 
\s1 David Marries Abigail
\b
\m 
\v 39 On hearing that \zwd Nabal|id="Q1709507" t="2"\zwd* was dead, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* said, “Blessed be the LORD, who has upheld my cause against the reproach of \zwd Nabal|id="Q1709507" t="2"\zwd* and has restrained \zwd His servant|Q41370\zwd* from evil. For the LORD has brought the wickedness of \zwd Nabal|id="Q1709507" t="2"\zwd* down upon his own head.” 
\b
\m Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* sent word to \zwd Abigail|Q4416778\zwd*, asking her to become his wife. 
\v 40 When his servants came to \zwd Abigail|Q4416778\zwd* at Carmel, they said, “\zwd David|Q41370\zwd* has sent us to take you as his wife.” 
\b
\m 
\v 41 She arose, bowed facedown, and said, “Here is \zwd your servant|Q4416778\zwd*, ready to serve and to wash the feet of \zwd my lord|Q41370\zwd*’s servants.” 
\b
\m 
\v 42 So \zwd Abigail|Q4416778\zwd* hurried and got on a donkey, and attended by five of her maidens, she followed \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s messengers and became his wife. 
\b
\m 
\v 43 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* had also married \zwd Ahinoam of Jezreel|Q400266\zwd*. So she and \zwd Abigail|Q4416778\zwd* were both his wives. 
\v 44 But \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* had given \zwd his daughter Michal|Q1341397\zwd*, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s wife, to \zwd Palti \f + \fr 25:44 \ft Palti is a variant of Paltiel; see 2 Samuel 3:15.\f* son of \zwd Laish|id="Q106096033" t="2"\zwd*|Q7128770\zwd*, who was from Gallim. 
\c 26
\s1 David Again Spares Saul
\m 
\v 1 Then the Ziphites came to \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* at Gibeah and said, “Is not \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* hiding on the hill of Hachilah, opposite Jeshimon?” 
\v 2 So \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, accompanied by three thousand chosen men of Israel, went down to the Wilderness of Ziph to search for \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* there. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* camped beside the road at the hill of Hachilah opposite Jeshimon, but \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* was living in the wilderness. When he realized that \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* had followed him there, 
\v 4 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* sent out spies to verify that \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* had arrived. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* set out and went to the place where \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* had camped. He saw the place where \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* and \zwd Abner son of \zwd Ner|id="Q132145387" t="2"\zwd*|Q1133337\zwd*, the general of his army, had lain down. \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* was lying inside the inner circle of the camp, with the troops camped around him. 
\v 6 And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* asked \zwd Ahimelech the Hittite|\zwd* and \zwd Abishai son of \zwd Zeruiah|\zwd*|Q1154317\zwd*, \zwd Joab|id="Q1141064" t="2"\zwd*’s brother, “Who will go down with me to \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* in the camp?” 
\b
\m “I will go with you,” answered \zwd Abishai|Q1154317\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 That night \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and \zwd Abishai|Q1154317\zwd* came to the troops, and \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* was lying there asleep in the inner circle of the camp, with his spear stuck in the ground by his head. And \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd* and the troops were lying around him. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 \zwd Abishai|Q1154317\zwd* said to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, “Today God has delivered your enemy into your hand. Now, therefore, please let me thrust the spear through him into the ground with one stroke. I will not need to strike him twice!” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 But \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* said to \zwd Abishai|Q1154317\zwd*, “Do not destroy him, for who can extend a hand against the LORD’s anointed and be guiltless?” 
\v 10 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* added, “As surely as the LORD lives, the LORD Himself will strike him down; either his day will come and he will die, or he will go into battle and perish. 
\v 11 But the LORD forbid that I should extend my hand against the LORD’s anointed. Instead, take the spear and water jug by his head, and let us go.” 
\b
\m 
\v 12 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* took the spear and water jug by \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*’s head, and they departed. No one saw them or knew about it, nor did anyone wake up; they all remained asleep, because a deep sleep from the LORD had fallen on them. 
\s1 David Reproves Abner
\b
\m 
\v 13 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* crossed to the other side and stood atop the mountain at a distance; there was a wide gulf between them. 
\v 14 And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* shouted to the troops and to \zwd Abner son of \zwd Ner|id="Q132145387" t="2"\zwd*|Q1133337\zwd*, “Will you not answer me, \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd*?” 
\b
\m “Who are you who calls to \zwd the king|Q28730\zwd*?” \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd* replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* said to \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd*, “You are a man, aren’t you? And who in Israel is your equal? Why then did you not protect \zwd your lord the king|Q28730\zwd* when one of the people came to destroy him? 
\v 16 This thing you have done is not good. As surely as the LORD lives, all of you deserve to die, since you did not protect \zwd your lord|Q28730\zwd*, the LORD’s anointed. Now look around. Where are \zwd the king|Q28730\zwd*’s spear and water jug that were by his head?” 
\b
\m 
\v 17 Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* recognized \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s voice and asked, “Is that your voice, \zwd David my son|Q41370\zwd*?” 
\b
\m “It is my voice, \zwd my lord and king|Q28730\zwd*,” \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* said. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 And he continued, “Why is \zwd my lord|Q28730\zwd* pursuing \zwd his servant|Q41370\zwd*? What have I done? What evil is in my hand? 
\v 19 Now please, may \zwd my lord the king|Q28730\zwd* hear the words of \zwd his servant|Q41370\zwd*: If the LORD has stirred you up against me, then may He accept an offering. But if men have done it, may they be cursed in the presence of the LORD! For today they have driven me away from sharing in the inheritance of the LORD, saying, ‘Go, serve other gods.’ 
\v 20 So do not let my blood fall to the ground far from the presence of the LORD. For \zwd the king of Israel|Q28730\zwd* has come out to look for a flea, like one who hunts a partridge in the mountains.” 
\s1 Saul Acknowledges His Sin
\b
\m 
\v 21 Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* replied, “I have sinned. Come back, \zwd David my son|Q41370\zwd*. I will never harm you again, because today you considered my life precious. I have played the fool and have committed a grave error!” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 “Here is \zwd the king|Q28730\zwd*’s spear,” \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* answered. “Let one of the young men come over and get it. 
\v 23 May the LORD repay every man for his righteousness and faithfulness. For the LORD delivered you into my hand today, but I would not extend my hand against the LORD’s anointed. 
\v 24 As surely as I valued your life today, so may the LORD value my life and rescue me from all trouble.” 
\b
\m 
\v 25 \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* said to him, “May you be blessed, \zwd David my son|Q41370\zwd*. You will accomplish great things and will surely prevail.” 
\b
\m So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* went on his way, and \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* returned home. 
\c 27
\s1 David and the Philistines
\m 
\v 1 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, however, said to himself, “One of these days now I will be swept away by the hand of \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*. There is nothing better for me than to escape to the land of the Philistines. Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* will stop searching for me all over Israel, and I will slip out of his hand.” 
\b
\m 
\v 2 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* set out with his six hundred men and went to \zwd Achish son of \zwd Maoch|\zwd*|Q167185\zwd*,\f + \fr 27:2 \ft Maoch is a variant of Maacah; see 1 Kings 2:39.\f* the king of Gath. 
\v 3 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and his men settled in Gath with \zwd Achish|Q167185\zwd*. Each man had his family with him, and \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* had \zwd his two wives|Q400266,Q4416778\zwd*: \zwd Ahinoam of Jezreel|Q400266\zwd* and \zwd Abigail of Carmel|Q4416778\zwd*, the widow of \zwd Nabal|id="Q1709507" t="2"\zwd*. 
\v 4 And when \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* learned that \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* had fled to Gath, he no longer searched for him. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* said to \zwd Achish|Q167185\zwd*, “If I have found favor in your eyes, let me be assigned a place in one of the outlying towns, so I can live there. For why should \zwd your servant|Q41370\zwd* live in the royal city with you?” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 That day \zwd Achish|Q167185\zwd* gave him Ziklag, and to this day it still belongs to the kings of Judah. 
\v 7 And the time that \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* lived in Philistine territory amounted to a year and four months. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 Now \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and his men went up and raided the Geshurites, the Girzites, and the Amalekites. (From ancient times these people had inhabited the land extending to Shur and Egypt.) 
\v 9 Whenever \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* attacked a territory, he did not leave a man or woman alive, but he took the flocks and herds, the donkeys, camels, and clothing. 
\b
\m Then he would return to \zwd Achish|Q167185\zwd*, 
\v 10 who would ask him, “What have you raided today?” 
\b
\m And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* would reply, “The Negev of Judah,” or “The Negev of Jerahmeel,” or “The Negev of the Kenites.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* did not leave a man or woman alive to be brought to Gath, for he said, “Otherwise they will report us, saying, ‘This is what \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* did.’” And this was \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s custom the whole time he lived in Philistine territory. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 So \zwd Achish|Q167185\zwd* trusted \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, thinking, “Since he has made himself an utter stench to his people Israel, he will be my servant forever.” 
\c 28
\s1 The Philistines Gather against Israel
\m 
\v 1 Now in those days the Philistines gathered their forces for warfare against Israel. So \zwd Achish|Q167185\zwd* said to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, “You must understand that you and your men are to go out to battle with me.” 
\b
\m 
\v 2 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* replied, “Then you will come to know what \zwd your servant|Q41370\zwd* can do.” 
\b
\m “Very well,” said \zwd Achish|Q167185\zwd*. “I will make you my bodyguard for life.” 
\b
\m 
\v 3 Now by this time \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* had died, and all Israel had mourned for him and buried him in Ramah, his own city. And \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* had removed the mediums and spiritists from the land. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 The Philistines gathered together and camped at Shunem, while \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* gathered all Israel and camped at Gilboa. 
\v 5 When \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* saw the Philistine army, he was afraid and trembled violently. 
\v 6 He inquired of the LORD, but the LORD did not answer him by dreams or Urim \f + \fr 28:6 \ft Literally Lights\f* or prophets. 
\s1 Saul and the Medium of Endor
\b
\m 
\v 7 Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* said to his servants, “Find me a woman who is a medium, so I can go and consult her.” 
\b
\m “There is \zwd a medium|Q1391553\zwd* at Endor,” his servants replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 So \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* disguised himself by putting on different clothes, and he set out with two of his men. They came to \zwd the woman|Q1391553\zwd* at night, and \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* said, “Consult a spirit for me. Bring up for me the one I name.” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 But \zwd the woman|Q1391553\zwd* replied, “Surely you know what \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* has done, how he has killed the mediums and spiritists in the land. Why have you set a trap to get me killed?” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* swore to her by the LORD: “As surely as the LORD lives, no punishment shall come upon you for this.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 “Whom shall I bring up for you?” \zwd the woman|Q1391553\zwd* asked. 
\b
\m “Bring up \zwd Samuel|id="Q6577515" t="2"\zwd*,” he replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 But when \zwd the woman|Q1391553\zwd* saw \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*, she cried out in a loud voice and said to \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, “Why have you deceived me? You are \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*!” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 “Do not be afraid,” \zwd the king|Q28730\zwd* replied. “What do you see?” 
\b
\m “I see a god \f + \fr 28:13 \ft Or I see a spirit or I see a divine being\f* coming up out of the earth,” \zwd the woman|Q1391553\zwd* answered. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 “What does he look like?” asked \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*. 
\b
\m “An old man is coming up,” she replied. “And he is wearing a robe.” 
\b
\m So \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* knew that it was \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*, and he bowed facedown in reverence. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 Then \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd* said to \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, “Why have you disturbed me by bringing me up?” 
\b
\m “I am deeply distressed,” replied \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*. “The Philistines are fighting against me, and God has turned away from me. He no longer answers me, either by prophets or by dreams. So I have called on you to tell me what to do.” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 “Why do you consult me,” asked \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*, “since the LORD has turned away from you and become your enemy? 
\v 17 He has done exactly what He spoke through me: The LORD has torn the kingdom out of your hand and given it to \zwd your neighbor David|Q41370\zwd*. 
\v 18 Because you did not obey the LORD or carry out His burning anger against Amalek, the LORD has done this to you today. 
\v 19 Moreover, the LORD will deliver Israel with you into the hand of the Philistines, and tomorrow \zwd you and your sons|\zwd* will be with me. And the LORD will deliver the army of Israel into the hand of the Philistines.” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 Immediately \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* fell flat on the ground, terrified by the words of \zwd Samuel|Q6577515\zwd*. And his strength was gone, because he had not eaten anything all that day and night. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 When \zwd the woman|Q1391553\zwd* came to \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* and saw how distraught he was, she said to him, “Look, \zwd your maidservant|Q1391553\zwd* has obeyed your voice. I took my life in my hands and did as you told me. 
\v 22 Now please listen to \zwd your servant|Q1391553\zwd* and let me set a morsel of bread before you so you may eat and have the strength to go on your way.” 
\b
\m 
\v 23 \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* refused, saying, “I will not eat.” But his servants joined \zwd the woman|Q1391553\zwd* in urging him, and he heeded their voice. He got up from the ground and sat on the bed. 
\b
\m 
\v 24 \zwd The woman|Q1391553\zwd* had a fattened calf at her house, and she quickly slaughtered it. She also took flour, kneaded it, and baked unleavened bread. 
\v 25 She served it to \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* and his servants, and they ate. And that night they got up and left. 
\c 29
\s1 The Philistines Reject David
\m 
\v 1 Now the Philistines brought all their forces together at Aphek, while Israel camped by the spring in Jezreel. 
\v 2 As the Philistine leaders marched out with their units of hundreds and thousands, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and his men marched behind them with \zwd Achish|Q167185\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 Then the commanders of the Philistines asked, “What about these Hebrews?” 
\b
\m \zwd Achish|Q167185\zwd* replied, “Is this not \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, the servant of \zwd King Saul of Israel|Q28730\zwd*? He has been with me all these days, even years, and from the day he defected until today I have found no fault in him.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 But the commanders of the Philistines were angry with \zwd Achish|Q167185\zwd* and told him, “Send that man back and let him return to the place you assigned him. He must not go down with us into battle only to become our adversary during the war. What better way for him to regain the favor of \zwd his master|Q28730\zwd* than with the heads of our men? 
\v 5 Is this not the \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* about whom they sing in their dances: 
\b
\q1 ‘\zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* has slain his thousands, 
\q2 and \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* his tens of thousands’?” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 So \zwd Achish|Q167185\zwd* summoned \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and told him, “As surely as the LORD lives, you have been upright, and it seems right in my sight that you should march in and out with me in the army, because I have found no fault in you from the day you came to me until this day. But you are not good in the sight of the leaders. 
\v 7 Therefore turn back now and go in peace, so that you will not do anything to displease the leaders of the Philistines.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 “But what have I done?” \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* replied. “What have you found against \zwd your servant|Q41370\zwd*, from the day I came to you until today, to keep me from going along to fight against the enemies of \zwd my lord the king|Q167185\zwd*?” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 \zwd Achish|Q167185\zwd* replied, “I know that you are as pleasing in my sight as an angel of God. But the commanders of the Philistines have said, ‘He must not go into battle with us.’ 
\v 10 Now then, get up early in the morning, along with \zwd your master|\zwd*’s servants who came with you, and go as soon as it is light.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and his men got up early in the morning to return to the land of the Philistines. And the Philistines went up to Jezreel. 
\c 30
\s1 The Amalekites Raid Ziklag
\m 
\v 1 On the third day \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and his men arrived in Ziklag, and the Amalekites had raided the Negev, attacked Ziklag, and burned it down. 
\v 2 They had taken captive the women and all \f + \fr 30:2 \ft LXX; Hebrew does not include and all.\f* who were there, both young and old. They had not killed anyone, but had carried them off as they went on their way. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 When \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and his men came to the city, they found it burned down and their wives and sons and daughters taken captive. 
\v 4 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and the troops with him lifted up their voices and wept until they had no strength left to weep. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 \zwd \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s two wives|Q400266,Q4416778\zwd*, \zwd Ahinoam of Jezreel|Q400266\zwd* and \zwd Abigail the widow of \zwd Nabal of Carmel|id="Q1709507" t="2"\zwd*|Q4416778\zwd*, had been taken captive. 
\v 6 And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* was greatly distressed because the people spoke of stoning him, because the soul of every man grieved for his sons and daughters. But \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* found strength in the LORD his God. 
\s1 David Destroys the Amalekites
\b
\m 
\v 7 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* said to \zwd Abiathar the priest|Q321804\zwd*, the son of \zwd Ahimelech|\zwd*, “Bring me the ephod.” 
\b
\m So \zwd Abiathar|Q321804\zwd* brought it to him, 
\v 8 and \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* inquired of the LORD: “Should I pursue these raiders? Will I overtake them?” 
\b
\m “Pursue them,” the LORD replied, “for you will surely overtake them and rescue the captives.” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and his six hundred men went to the Brook of Besor, where some stayed behind 
\v 10 because two hundred men were too exhausted to cross the brook. But \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and four hundred men continued in pursuit. 
\b
\m 
\v 11 Now his men found an Egyptian in the field and brought him to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*. They gave the man water to drink and food to eat—
\v 12 a piece of a fig cake and two clusters of raisins. So he ate and was revived, for he had not had any food or water for three days and three nights. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* asked him, “To whom do you belong, and where are you from?” 
\b
\m “I am an Egyptian,” he replied, “the slave of an Amalekite. My master abandoned me three days ago when I fell ill. 
\v 14 We raided the Negev of the Cherethites, the territory of Judah, and the Negev of Caleb, and we burned down Ziklag.” 
\b
\m 
\v 15 “Will you lead me to these raiders?” \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* asked. 
\b
\m And the man replied, “Swear to me by God that you will not kill me or deliver me into the hand of my master, and I will lead you to them.” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 So he led \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* down, and there were the Amalekites spread out over all the land, eating, drinking, and celebrating the great amount of plunder they had taken from the land of the Philistines and the land of Judah. 
\v 17 And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* struck them down from twilight until the evening of the next day. Not a man escaped, except four hundred young men who fled, riding off on camels. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* recovered everything the Amalekites had taken, including \zwd his two wives|Q400266,Q4416778\zwd*. 
\v 19 Nothing was missing, young or old, son or daughter, or any of the plunder the Amalekites had taken. \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* brought everything back. 
\v 20 And he took all the flocks and herds, which his men drove ahead of the other livestock, calling out, “This is \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s plunder!” 
\s1 The Spoils Are Divided
\b
\m 
\v 21 When \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* came to the two hundred men who had been too exhausted to follow him and who were left behind at the Brook of Besor, they came out to meet him and the troops with him. As \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* approached the men, he greeted them, 
\v 22 but all the wicked and worthless men among those who had gone with \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* said, “Because they did not go with us, we will not share with them the plunder we recovered, except for each man’s wife and children. They may take them and go.” 
\b
\m 
\v 23 But \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* said, “My brothers, you must not do this with what the LORD has given us. He has protected us and delivered into our hands the raiders who came against us. 
\v 24 Who will listen to your proposal? The share of the one who went to battle will match the share of the one who stayed with the supplies. They will share alike.” 
\b
\m 
\v 25 And so it has been from that day forward. \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* established this statute and ordinance for Israel to this very day. 
\b
\m 
\v 26 When \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* arrived in Ziklag, he sent some of the plunder to his friends, the elders of Judah, saying, “Here is a gift for you from the plunder of the LORD’s enemies.” 
\v 27 He sent gifts to those in Bethel, Ramoth Negev, and Jattir; 
\v 28 to those in Aroer, Siphmoth, and Eshtemoa; 
\v 29 to those in Racal and in the cities of the Jerahmeelites and Kenites; 
\v 30 to those in Hormah, Bor-ashan, and Athach; 
\v 31 and to those in Hebron and in all the places where \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and his men had roamed. 
\c 31
\s1 Saul’s Overthrow and Death
\r (2 Samuel 1:1–16; 1 Chronicles 10:1–6)
\m 
\v 1 Now the Philistines fought against Israel, and the men of Israel fled before them, and many fell slain on Mount Gilboa. 
\b
\m 
\v 2 The Philistines hotly pursued \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* and his sons, and they killed \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*’s sons \zwd Jonathan|Q74891\zwd*, \zwd Abinadab|Q6039896\zwd*, and \zwd Malchishua|Q132132935\zwd*. 
\v 3 \zdate|Q28730:P570\*When the battle intensified against \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, the archers overtook him and wounded him critically. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Then \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* said to his armor-bearer, “Draw your sword and run me through with it, or these uncircumcised men will come and run me through and torture me!” 
\b
\m But his armor-bearer was terrified and refused to do it. So \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* took his own sword and fell on it. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 When his armor-bearer saw that \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* was dead, he too fell on his own sword and died with him. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 So \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, \zwd his three sons|Q74891,Q6039896,Q132132935\zwd*, his armor-bearer, and all his men died together that same day.\zdate|Q28730:P570\* 
\s1 The Philistines Possess the Towns
\r (1 Chronicles 10:7–10)
\b
\m 
\v 7 When the Israelites along the valley and those on the other side of the Jordan saw that the army of Israel had fled and that \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* and \zwd his sons|Q74891,Q6039896,Q132132935\zwd* had died, they abandoned their cities and ran away. So the Philistines came and occupied their cities. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 The next day, when the Philistines came to strip the dead, they found \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* and \zwd his three sons|Q74891,Q6039896,Q132132935\zwd* fallen on Mount Gilboa. 
\v 9 They cut off \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*’s head, stripped off his armor, and sent messengers throughout the land of the Philistines to proclaim the news in the temples of their idols and among their people. 
\v 10 They put his armor in the temple of the Ashtoreths and hung his body on the wall of Beth-shan. 
\s1 Jabesh-gilead’s Tribute to Saul
\r (1 Chronicles 10:11–14)
\b
\m 
\v 11 When the people of Jabesh-gilead heard what the Philistines had done to \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd*, 
\v 12 all their men of valor set out, journeyed all night, and retrieved the bodies of \zwd Saul|Q28730\zwd* and \zwd his sons|Q74891,Q6039896,Q132132935\zwd* from the wall of Beth-shan. 
\b
\m When they arrived at Jabesh, they burned the bodies there. 
\v 13 Then they took their bones and buried them under the tamarisk tree in Jabesh, and they fasted seven days. 
